\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Nearly all abortions become illegal in Arizona\b0\par\par\uc2 Gloria Oladipo and agencies \par \uc2 705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2022\par 13:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Several clinics halt procedure as dual measures, including 19th-century ban with no exception for rape or incest, take effect\par \par \uc2 Almost all abortions became illegal in Arizona on Saturday, after a new law banning abortions after the 15th week of pregnancy took effect and a judge lifted an almost 50-year-old injunction that blocked a near-total ban on abortions from being enforced in the state.\par \par \uc2 Judge Kellie Johnson of Pima county\u8217\'20\'19s superior court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/sep/23/arizona-abortion-ban-clinics-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released a ruling on Friday}}}\uc2  that allowed the enforcement of the decades-old ban, a day before a new law that would ban most procedures after 15 weeks was scheduled to take effect, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2022/09/24/arizona-abortion-law-judge-johnson-brnovich/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported the Washington Post}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law Johnson reinstated dates from 1864 and bans all abortions with no exception for rape or incest. The only exception involves a recipient whose life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 The law was later updated and codified in 1901, before the 1973 \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision known as Roe v Wade that established nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Many states failed to update their laws after the provision of those federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, which the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current conservative majority eliminated in June.\par \par \uc2 Immediately after Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, several Arizona clinics that provided abortions stopped carrying out the procedure to avoid criminal charges for their medical professionals, forcing almost all patients in need of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 Those who have already stopped offering abortions included Planned Parenthood along with two other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, the Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 Under Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, doctors or other healthcare professionals who terminate pregnancies could face between two and five years in prison.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and Democratic legislators condemned the new law in Arizona as well as Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s ruling.\par \par \uc2 The president and CEO of the Arizona branch of Planned Parenthood, Brittany Fonteno, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PPArizona/status/1573469893055348752"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called the ban}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Carchaic\u8221\'20\'1D and said it was \u8220\'20\'1Csending Arizonians back nearly 150 years\u8221\'20\'1D, referring to when the law was first written, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/local/arizona/2022/09/23/arizona-ban-on-nearly-all-abortions-in-effect-judge-rules/8075792001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Arizona Republic}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Arizona senator Krysten Sinema {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenatorSinema/status/1573488376438726664"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called out Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s ruling}}}\uc2  on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , writing in part: \u8220\'20\'1CA woman\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare decisions should be between her, her family, and her doctor. Today\u8217\'20\'19s decision removes basic rights Arizona women have relied upon for over a century and endangers their health, safety, and wellbeing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s other US senator, Mark Kelly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenMarkKelly/status/1573464705359806464"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on Twitter}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CRepealing Roe v Wade set Arizona women\u8217\'20\'19s rights back decades. This decision sets them back 158 years, to before Arizona was even a state. I won\u8217\'20\'19t stop until we restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights so my granddaughter can have the same freedoms my grandmother did.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s ruling has also caused confusion statewide, with some calling for the enforcement of the harsher ban codified in 1901 and others wanting only the 15-week ban to be enforced, reported the Post.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona attorney general, \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 , who filed to have the injunction blocking the older ban lifted, has argued that the harsher of the two laws will take precedent, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/23/us/arizona-abortion-ban.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports the New York Times}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , has stated the 15-week ban will be followed, with a representative of his office telling the Times that the governor is proud to have signed the ban. However, Ducey has not clarified whether the more restrictive law will be enforced.\par \par \uc2 Johnson, for her part, has indicated that the more restrictive law should be followed versus the 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost recently in 2022, the legislature enacted a 15-week gestational age limitation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D the judge wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CThe legislature expressly included in the session law that the 15-week gestational age limitation\u8221\'20\'1D would not \u8220\'20\'1Crepeal\u8221\'20\'1D the previous ban.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts have also warned that the previously approved 15-week ban may no longer be tenable, with Loyola Marymount University family law professor Kaiponanea Matsumura telling the Post that Brnovich\u8217\'20\'19s position as attorney general \u8220\'20\'1Copens the door to prosecutions under that law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Arizona is now among at least 14 states which have outlawed most abortions. Several more have similar bans that are temporarily blocked amid legal wrangling over whether or not they can be enforced.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220926ei9q00238\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
