\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump lawyers reject \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s arguments against special master \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein in Washington \par \uc2 5,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2022\par 20:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump team submit response to justice department appealSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 The legal battle over documents seized by the government from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Mar-a-Lago resort continued, with the former president\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f39748f0804237ace7782#block-631f39748f0804237ace7782"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejecting the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s efforts}}}\uc2  to convince a federal judge to let them continue reviewing the materials. The filing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f3d448f0804237ace77ca#block-631f3d448f0804237ace77ca"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 avoided questions of whether what was taken was indeed protected}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 despite Trump\u8217\'20\'19s assertions that he had declassified everything that was found before leaving office.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a rundown of what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Trump also rejected the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s nominees for special master, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f824d8f08b0bfb4b6b6a8#block-631f824d8f08b0bfb4b6b6a8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 though declined to say why publicly}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Republicans are considering{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f42d18f087fa006e160fc#block-631f42d18f087fa006e160fc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigating the security failures}}}\uc2  that led up to the January 6 attack if they win control of the House.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Democratic candidates are polling strongly in some swing states \u8211\'20\'13 but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f504e8f08b0bfb4b6b4bc#block-631f504e8f08b0bfb4b6b4bc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an analysis from The New York Times}}}\uc2  warns it could be an illusion.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A new poll indicates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f35d78f08384c60156243#block-631f35d78f08384c60156243"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sizable minorities of Americans}}}\uc2  would be ok with unelected leaders, and many would also be ok with the government overruling minority ethnicities and religions.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Democrats have spent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f6f248f08f46fd168d58b#block-631f6f248f08f46fd168d58b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost $19 million across eight states}}}\uc2  in controversial efforts to promote rightwing Republican candidates, believing them to be weaker in the November midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in Congress, lawmakers may be back in Washington but not much has happened \u8211\'20\'13 yet.\par \par \uc2 The Senate will soon confirm president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s 80th judicial nominee, CNN reports, as Democrats look to make their mark on the federal judiciary:\par \par \uc2 enltrAfter Salvador Mendoza is confirmed today to the Ninth Circuit, he will be the 80th Biden judicial nominee confirmed -- something Schumer just said amounts to more than Trump, Obama, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  at similar points in their presidencies\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Manu Raju (@mkraju) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mkraju/status/1569408436315504650?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The chamber\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has also confirmed that work is ongoing on a new government funding bill, and on finding 10 GOP senators willing to sign on to a bill codifying same-sex marriage rights, according to Politico:\par \par \uc2 enltrSchumer updates: Senate on verge of confirming 80th judge of Biden presidency\par \par \uc2 Bipartisan group still working on same-sex marriage bill, trying to get GOP votes\par \par \uc2 Work ongoing on bill to fund government past Sept. 30\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Burgess Everett (@burgessev) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/burgessev/status/1569406944397463555?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you thought Fulton was a good county to bring your crime to, to bring your violence to, you are wrong.\u8221\'20\'1D So declares \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , the district attorney in Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Fulton County, in the opening lines of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/12/us/fani-t-willis-trump-atlanta.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a profile published in The New York Times}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the statement was made in the context of a gang racketeering case, the piece makes clear it could also be said about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the people from Georgia and elsewhere who helped in his attempt to meddle with the state\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 election result. Willis has convened the special grand jury that is investigating that campaign, which has subpoenaed Trump allies including attorney \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , Republican senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and others. The piece doesn\u8217\'20\'19t contain much new details about what the grand jurors have learned, but it makes clear the scope of the ongoing investigation, which some analysts have warned is a source of legal peril for the former president.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from the profile:\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Ms. Willis has called dozens of witnesses to testify before a special grand jury investigating efforts to undo Mr. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s defeat, including a number of prominent pro-Trump figures who traveled, against their will, from other states. It was long arm of the law stuff, and it emphasized how her investigation, though playing out more than 600 miles from Washington, D.C., is no sideshow.\par \par \uc2 Rather, the Georgia inquiry has emerged as one of the most consequential legal threats to the former president, and it is already being shaped by Ms. Willis\u8217\'20\'19s distinct and forceful personality and her conception of how a local prosecutor should do her job. Her comfort in the public eye stands in marked contrast to the low-key approach of another Trump legal pursuer, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Willis, 50, a Democrat, is the first Black woman to lead Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s largest district attorney\u8217\'20\'19s office. In her 19 years as a prosecutor, she has led more than 100 jury trials and handled hundreds of murder cases. Since she became chief prosecutor, her office\u8217\'20\'19s conviction rate has stood at close to 90 percent, according to a spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 Her experience is the source of her confidence, which appears unshaken by the scrutiny \u8212\'20\'14 and criticism \u8212\'20\'14 the Trump case has brought.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been a new development in the ongoing legal wrangling over the \uc2 FBI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s search of Mar-a-Lago and the documents found there. As The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Hugo Lowell reports, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys have objected to the candidates for special master proposed by the justice department:\par \par \uc2 enltrNew: Trump objects to proposed Special Master candidates proposed by DOJ \u8212\'20\'14 says it would offer rationale to judge privately {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1zfTaUzUt6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/1zfTaUzUt6}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Hugo Lowell (@hugolowell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hugolowell/status/1569398750732517377?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrTrump objects even to former US district court judge Barbara Jones, who was the special master in the \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  case in 2018 and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  in 2021\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Hugo Lowell (@hugolowell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hugolowell/status/1569400163877142528?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Last week, a federal judge granted Trump\u8217\'20\'19s request for a special master to review documents taken by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from Mar-a-Lago to screen for privileged material. The decision stopped the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to review the seized documents, and the justice department is appealing it.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Twenty-one years after 9/11, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/9-11-attacks-khalid-sheikh-mohammed-guantanamo-bay-plea-deals/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CBS News}}}\uc2  reports that five of the defendants held at Guantanamo Bay for alleged involvement in the attacks are negotiating plea deals with the government.\par \par \uc2 The defendants have been incarcerated for years due to disputes over what evidence can be used in the court and, more recently, the Covid-19 pandemic. Plea deals would resolve several of their cases and likely result in lengthy jail sentences, but \uc2 CBS\uc2  reports some relatives of those killed in the attack oppose such agreements.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe families are outraged,\u8221\'20\'1D said Debra Burlingame, whose brother was among those killed when hijackers steered his plane into the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from \uc2 CBS\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The chief defendant is Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, the self-described architect of 9/11. The other four defendants are Ramzi Binalshibh, Mustafa Ahmed al-Hawsawi, Walid bin Attash and Ammar al-Baluchi.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of a plea deal has angered the families of some 9/11 victims, including Debra Burlingame, whose brother, pilot Charles \u8220\'20\'1CChic\u8221\'20\'1D Burlingame, was killed when al Qaeda terrorists took over his plane, American Airlines Flight 77, and crashed it into the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t have remains for weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D his sister Debra Burlingame told CBS News. \u8220\'20\'1CWe were constantly saying to each other, \u8216\'20\'18What would Chic want? What would Chic do?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Burlingame said she has been in touch with other 9/11 families.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe families are outraged,\u8221\'20\'1D she said of the possibility of plea deals. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t want closure, they want justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another group, 9/11 Families for Peaceful Tomorrows, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peacefultomorrows.org/a-primer-on-the-9-11-military-commission-at-guantanamo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has said}}}\uc2  that a guilty plea and agreement not to appeal the sentence \u8220\'20\'1Cwould be partly in recognition of the torture each of the defendants experienced\u8221\'20\'1D and bring \u8220\'20\'1Csome measure of judicial finality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll five defendants and the government are all engaged in good faith negotiations, with the idea of bringing this trial which has become a forever trial to an end,\u8221\'20\'1D said James Connell, a defense attorney for al-Baluchi.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMr. al-Baluchi\u8217\'20\'19s number one priority is obtaining medical care for his torture,\u8221\'20\'1D Connell continued. \u8220\'20\'1CIn order to get that medical care, he is willing to plead guilty to a substantial sentence at Guantanamo in exchange for a guarantee of medical care and dropping the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Democratic candidate for Senate in Pennsylvania has put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the center of his pitch to voters, in the latest sign the party is banking on the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade to drum up support in the midterms. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/richardluscombe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Richard Luscombe}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has placed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the top of his agenda to capture Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s Senate seat in November, telling supporters at a raucous rally on Sunday: \u8220\'20\'1CWomen are the reason we can win. Don\u8217\'20\'19t piss off women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Democrat was targeting comments made by his Republican opponent Mehmet Oz in May that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/dr-oz-says-abortion-is-still-murder-at-any-stage-of-pregnancy?ref=home"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  at any stage of pregnancy was \u8220\'20\'1Cmurder\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oz, in keeping with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/12/republicans-soften-staunch-anti-abortion-stance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent trend among Republican candidates}}}\uc2 , has attempted to soften his extremist position as the fall\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections draw closer, insisting that he now believes in exceptions for rape, incest and the health of the woman.\par \par \uc2 But Oz\u8217\'20\'19s rival was uncompromising in his criticism during Sunday\u8217\'20\'19s rally at a community college in rural Pennsylvania attended by several thousand supporters, including a large number of women in pink \u8220\'20\'1CFetterwoman\u8221\'20\'1D T-shirts.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/12/john-fetterman-pennsylvania-rally-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Women are the reason we can win,\u8217\'20\'19 John Fetterman says at Pennsylvania rally}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 The justice department has brought charges against a Texas woman who left threatening voice messages on the phone of a judge involved in disputes around documents taken by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Mar-a-Lago resort, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/us-charges-woman-over-threats-against-judge-trump-special-master-case-2022-09-12/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reuters reports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been disqualified long ago, and he\u8217\'20\'19s marked for assassination. You\u8217\'20\'19re helping him, ma\u8217\'20\'19am,\u8221\'20\'1D said one of the voicemails, which was allegedly left by Tiffani Shea Gish of the Houston area for Aileen Cannon, a US district judge in Fort Pierce, Florida. Cannon, who was appointed to the bench by Trump, last week granted his request for a special master to review documents taken from Mar-a-Lago as part of the government\u8217\'20\'19s investigation into whether the former president unlawfully retained government secrets.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from Reuters\u8217\'20\'19 report:\par \par \uc2 Gish faces two criminal charges - influencing a federal official by threat and interstate communications with a threat to kidnap or injure.\par \par \uc2 Cannon, who was appointed to the bench in 2020 by Trump, ruled last week that she was granting the former president\u8217\'20\'19s request over the Justice Department\u8217\'20\'19s objections to install a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial master\u8221\'20\'1D to review the seized records to weed out possibly privileged materials.\par \par \uc2 The complaint said that on Sept. 3, Cannon forwarded three separate voicemails from Gish, who referred to herself in some of them as \u8220\'20\'1CEvelyn Salt,\u8221\'20\'1D to the U.S. Marshals Service.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been disqualified long ago, and he\u8217\'20\'19s marked for assassination. You\u8217\'20\'19re helping him, ma\u8217\'20\'19am,\u8221\'20\'1D one of the voicemails said, according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s marked for assassination and so are you,\u8221\'20\'1D the caller also said, while including an expletive.\par \par \uc2 After \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents identified a cellphone number associated with the voicemails, they interviewed Gish at her home, the complaint stated. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  said she admitted to leaving the voicemails and confirmed that the number belonged to her and no one else had access to the cellphone.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 One of the most controversial electoral tactics Democrats have deployed recently is spending money to elevate rightwing candidates in Republican primaries, the logic being that more extreme nominees will hurt the GOP in the November midterm elections.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/09/12/democrats-interfere-republican-primaries/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Washington Post has tallied}}}\uc2  the money spent on these candidates in an analysis released today, and found it adds up to almost $19 million across eight states, but could go up to $53 million if spending in Illinois is factored in. In that state, Democrats spent massively to help a Republican who said party leaders in the state should not have told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to leave the White House when his term was up.\par \par \uc2 The tactic is controversial because it could backfire and result in Republicans who hold extreme views \u8211\'20\'13 such as that the 2020 election was stolen \u8211\'20\'13 elected to major offices in the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from the Post\u8217\'20\'19s story:\par \par \uc2 The approach often involves TV ads suggesting that a far-right GOP candidate is too conservative for a state or district and drawing attention to the candidate\u8217\'20\'19s hard line views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns and former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 messages that resonate with conservative primary voters. In other cases, Democrats have run ads attacking GOP candidates seen as tougher to defeat in general elections in ways that could erode support for them in Republican primaries.\par \par \uc2 Total Democratic spending rises to roughly $53 million when a ninth state, Illinois, is added. There, the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  and the campaign of Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  (D) spent a combined $34.5 million successfully elevating a GOP candidate who has said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cappalling\u8221\'20\'1D that party leaders in Illinois wanted Trump to concede the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats explain their actions by saying they are simply getting a jump on attacking Republican candidates for the general election, while others openly acknowledge trying to secure weaker competition in the fall. But there is little dispute about the effect of altering the Republican primaries in ways that could affect the November matchups.\par \par \uc2 As primary season nears its Tuesday endpoint, Democrats are giving the strategy one more try in New Hampshire, in two congressional races. In the Republican Senate primary, Senate Majority PAC, a group aligned with Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Charles E. Schumer\uc2  (D-N.Y.), is spending $3.2 million on ads that effectively enhance the candidacy in the GOP primary of ret. Gen. Don Bolduc, by portraying his more moderate rival, state Senate President Chuck Morse, who has trailed in GOP primary polls to Bolduc, as beholden to the party establishment.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Neck and neck in Ohio senate\par \par \uc2 It is only one poll but a recent survey in the Ohio senate race shows Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  basically level with right-wing Trump ally and famous author JD Vance. Ohio is a state has has been drifting more red and so the news fits in with a revival of Democrat fortunes over the past month.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3639027-tim-ryan-j-d-vance-locked-in-tight-ohio-senate-race-poll/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Hill has more details}}}\uc2  : A USA TODAY Network Ohio/Suffolk University poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.suffolk.edu/-/media/suffolk/documents/academics/research-at-suffolk/suprc/polls/other-states/2022/9_12_2022_ohio_embargoed_marginals.pdf?la=en&hash=907A8FE77D028CA5416C761EB0631CC973695F53"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released Monday}}}\uc2  found 47 percent of Ohio general election voters said they would vote or lean toward Ryan if the Senate election were held today, while 46 percent said they could back Vance.\par \par \uc2 Six percent of respondents in the poll said they were undecided, while 1 percent said they would support someone else. The slim margin between the two leading candidates falls within the poll\u8217\'20\'19s margin of error of 4.4 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 The legal battle over documents seized by the government from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Mar-a-Lago resort continued, with the former president\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f39748f0804237ace7782#block-631f39748f0804237ace7782"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejecting the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s efforts}}}\uc2  to convince a federal judge to let them continue reviewing the materials. The filing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f3d448f0804237ace77ca#block-631f3d448f0804237ace77ca"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 avoided questions of whether what was taken was indeed protected}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 despite Trump\u8217\'20\'19s assertions that he had declassified everything that was found before leaving office.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a rundown of what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Republicans are considering{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f42d18f087fa006e160fc#block-631f42d18f087fa006e160fc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigating the security failures}}}\uc2  that led up to the January 6 attack if they win control of the House.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Democratic candidates are polling strongly in some swing states \u8211\'20\'13 but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f504e8f08b0bfb4b6b4bc#block-631f504e8f08b0bfb4b6b4bc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an analysis from The New York Times}}}\uc2  warns it could be an illusion.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A new poll indicates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/12/trump-mar-a-lago-special-master-fbi-biden-latest?page=with:block-631f35d78f08384c60156243#block-631f35d78f08384c60156243"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sizable minorities of Americans}}}\uc2  would be ok with unelected leaders, and many would also be ok with the government overruling minority ethnicities and religions.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is planning to ask Washington for more long-distance weapons to continue its offensive into Russian-held territory, including a missile system its ally had held off on providing for fears it could provoke Moscow, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ukraine-signals-major-weapons-request-for-long-term-offensive-against-russia-11662998386"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Wall Street Journal reports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Citing a document shared with US lawmakers, the Journal reports Ukraine will ask for 29 types of weapons and ammunition systems, including anti-ship missiles, drones and tanks. It will also ask for the Army Tactical Missile System, a long-range weapon that Washington fears could be used to strike Russian territory and start a war with Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s western allies.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from the Journal\u8217\'20\'19s report:\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, which has dispatched more than $15 billion worth of weapons and other security assistance to Ukraine, has declined to provide that system over concerns Ukraine could use it to strike Russian territory and spark a wider conflict with the West.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s list of requirements for \u8220\'20\'1Coffensive operations\u8221\'20\'1D includes 29 types of weapon systems and ammunition. Among them are tanks, drones, artillery systems; more Harpoon antiship missiles; and 2,000 missiles for the High Mobility Artillery Rocket System, or Himars, which the United States began providing earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s requests come as its forces have routed Russian troops in northeastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It follows the recent publication of a strategy statement by Valeriy Zaluzhny, the commander in chief of Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s force, and Mykhailo Zabrodsky, a member of the Ukrainian parliament and a senior military officer who led the most significant Ukrainian counterattack in the 2014 war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 They argued that Russia has long-range cruise missiles that greatly outdistance the systems in the Ukrainian inventory. A turning point could come if the Ukrainians also had longer-range systems, they argued, specifically mentioning the ATACMS.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump may be in hot water, legally speaking, for allegedly taking government secrets with him when he left the White House, but as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/ramon-antonio-vargas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ramon Antonio Vargas}}}\uc2  reports, a new book reveals he never wanted to leave in the first place:\par \par \uc2 In the days after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  defeated him in the 2020 election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  told an aide he was \u8220\'20\'1Cjust not going to leave\u8221\'20\'1D the White House, according to a new book on his presidency and its chaotic aftermath.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re never leaving,\u8221\'20\'1D he vowed to another aide, says the book from \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporter Maggie Haberman titled Confidence Man: The Making of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Breaking of America. \u8220\'20\'1CHow can you leave when you won an election?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/09/12/politics/trump-vowed-to-stay-in-white-house-haberman-book/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN}}}\uc2 , where Haberman also serves as a political analyst, said Monday it reviewed reporting for the book \u8211\'20\'13 set for a 4 October release \u8211\'20\'13 and published new details on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s insistence that he intended to stay at the White House despite his electoral loss to Biden.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/12/trump-white-house-election-defeat-biden-book"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump threatened not to leave White House after election loss, book says}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democrats have grown increasingly upbeat about their prospects in the upcoming midterms, pointing to the outrage over the end of nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, declining gas prices and the passage of major pieces of legislation intended to help Americans.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, polls in recent battleground states have shown Democratic Senate candidates ahead of their Republicans challengers, including in closely divided races such as those in Wisconsin, Nevada and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But what if it was all a mirage? Polls in recent presidential races have been far off the mark in crucial states, and in 2020, overestimated Democrats strength among voters. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  today {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/12/upshot/polling-midterms-warning.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published an analysis}}}\uc2  that takes into account these polling errors, and applies them to the latest surveys coming out in battleground Senate races across the country. The analysis finds that Democratic candidates may be far weaker than they appear in states like Nevada and Georgia, and actually running behind Republicans in North Carolina, Ohio and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 If the data is correct, here is what the Times says it would mean for Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 quest to maintain control of the Senate:\par \par \uc2 The apparent Democratic edge in Senate races in Wisconsin, North Carolina and Ohio would evaporate. To take the chamber, Republicans would need any two of Georgia, Arizona, Nevada or Pennsylvania. With Democrats today well ahead in Pennsylvania and Arizona, the fight for control of the chamber would come down to very close races in Nevada and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of who was favored, the race for Senate control would be extremely competitive. Republican control of the House would seem to be a foregone conclusion.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Outrage over the end of Roe v Wade has pushed some Republicans to soften their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances as they look to court voters in the upcoming midterm elections, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/maya-yang"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maya Yang}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 A growing number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  are changing their positions on abortions since the fall of Roe v Wade as midterm elections approach in the US, signaling a softened shift from their previously staunch anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances.\par \par \uc2 Since the supreme court overturned the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, many Republicans are adopting more compromised positions in attempts to win votes in key states through a slew of changes in messaging on websites, advertisements and public statements.\par \par \uc2 The moves comes amid a ferocious backlash to the decision that has seen Democrat hopes in the midterm elections revived and even see a solidly red state like Kansas vote in a referendum to keep some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/12/republicans-soften-staunch-anti-abortion-stance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A wakeup call\u8217\'20\'19: more Republicans are softening staunch anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  stance}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Following a historic term that will be remembered for a series of conservative decisions including the end of nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s chief justice has responded to critics that say the jurors are risking their legitimacy, \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/maya-yang"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maya Yang}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has defended his conservative-leaning bench from attacks over its decision in June to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn federal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 , as US vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  launched a fierce attack on what {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/09/kamala-harris-activist-supreme-court-roe-v-wade-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she called}}}\uc2  today\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cactivist court\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Roberts, in his first public appearance since the bombshell ruling to overturn the landmark 1973 Roe v Wade decision, warned against linking contentious decisions with court legitimacy, saying at an event on Friday night: \u8220\'20\'1CThe court has always decided controversial cases and decisions have always been subject to intense criticism, and that is entirely appropriate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But in her first sit-down interview with a TV network since becoming vice-president, Harris told NBC News that she now believes the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court}}}\uc2  is an \u8220\'20\'1Cactivist court\u8221\'20\'1D after the institution took away nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/sep/11/john-roberts-defends-supreme-court-kamala-harris-abortion-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Roberts defends supreme court as Kamala Harris lashes out at Roe ruling}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee is unlikely to continue its work in 2023, particularly if Republicans win a majority in the House of Representatives. But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean the party is going to ignore the attack entirely.\par \par \uc2 Politico reports that the GOP is considering launching its own January 6 committee if it wins control of the House, but this one will avoid looking into the actions of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and instead focus on security breaches that led to the attack. Here\u8217\'20\'19s more {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/09/12/house-gop-trump-free-jan-6-00056014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from Politico\u8217\'20\'19s report}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 While past investigations by the Senate and \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  inspector general have thoroughly explored many of those areas and made a laundry list of recommendations to bolster security, not to mention a forthcoming report from the Democratic-run Jan. 6 select committee, House GOP lawmakers are determined to run their own, Trump-free inquiry.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a contradictory turn for a conference that has struggled for a successful message defending Trump against revelations already uncovered by the select panel, instead largely urging Washington to move on. But after two years of being on the outside looking in \u8212\'20\'14Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy pulled his picks from the panel after Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  rejected two of them \u8212\'20\'14 House Republicans are eager to flip the script.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s been very well-documented that there were significant intelligence and communications failures on Jan. 6. It\u8217\'20\'19s not the first time we\u8217\'20\'19ve had those issues,\u8221\'20\'1D Rep. Kelly Armstrong (R-N.D.), who voted to certify President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Electoral College win, said in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to stop that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Though House Republicans have stayed almost completely out of the ongoing Jan. 6 probe, they\u8217\'20\'19ve quietly laid the groundwork for changes to the Capitol\u8217\'20\'19s security apparatus that would take effect much more quickly than the investigations they plan to mount.\par \par \uc2 For example, they want to get rid of the metal detectors installed around the House floor after Jan. 6 that have fueled GOP ire and resulted in hefty fines for lawmakers who tried to dodge them. They are also eager to reopen the Capitol complex, which still has restrictions in place after shuttering at the start of the pandemic. Armstrong noted while many of his colleagues will look back at Jan. 6, his focus is on how the building operates moving forward on a \u8220\'20\'1Cgeneral 11 a.m. on a Wednesday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.15pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.flsd.618763/gov.uscourts.flsd.618763.84.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In their filing}}}\uc2 , lawyers for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have downplayed the security risks of storing documents at Mar-a-Lago, and said there\u8217\'20\'19s no evidence any secrets stored there were improperly shared.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no indication any purported \u8216\'20\'18classified records\u8217\'20\'19 were disclosed to anyone. Indeed, it appears such \u8216\'20\'18classified records,\u8217\'20\'19 along with the other seized materials, were principally located in storage boxes in a locked room at Mar-a-Lago, a secure, controlled access compound utilized regularly to conduct the official business of the United States during the Trump Presidency, which to this day is monitored by the \uc2 United States Secret Service\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D they wrote in the 21-page document.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers also avoid the question of whether any of the documents found at Mar-a-Lago were actually classified or otherwise meant to be kept secret. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Government has not proven these records remain classified. That issue is to be determined later.\u8221\'20\'1D However, that statement appears to contradict Trump, who has argued that he declassified the documents found at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump lawyers argue against government attempt to continue viewing Mar-a-Lago documents\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have submitted their counterargument to the justice department\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to halt a federal judge\u8217\'20\'19s order preventing them from reviewing documents taken from Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 The filing is the latest in the squabble over the special master Trump wants appointed to sift through the documents, which the government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/08/justice-department-appeals-special-master-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has objected to}}}\uc2  because it stops them from reading the materials seized from the former president\u8217\'20\'19s south Florida estate.\par \par \uc2 You can read the filing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.flsd.618763/gov.uscourts.flsd.618763.84.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 More Americans than you might think have an affinity for unelected leaders, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/09/12/two-americas-index-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new data from the Axios-Ipsos Two Americas Index}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The survey shows that anti-democratic views exist among minorities of both Republicans and Democrats, with 33% of voters surveyed agreeing with the statement \u8220\'20\'1CStrong, unelected leaders are better than weak elected ones\u8221\'20\'1D. That includes 31% of Democrats and 42% of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe findings from this poll shatter the myth that Americans overwhelmingly agree on a common set of democratic values around checks and balances on elected leaders, protection of minority rights and freedom of speech,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/09/12/two-americas-index-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Axios wrote}}}\uc2 , adding that the data also runs counter to president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s insistence that Trump supporters pose the biggest threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 Democrats outpolled Republicans on the question of \u8220\'20\'1CPresidents should be able to remove judges whose decisions go against the national interest\u8221\'20\'1D, with 29% of GOP voters agreeing with that against 42% of Democrats \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps a reflection of outrage over recent decisions by the conservative-dominated supreme court.\par \par \uc2 A significant 38% of voters polled agreed with the statement \u8220\'20\'1CGovernment should side with the majority over ethnic/religious minority rights\u8221\'20\'1D, with Republicans and Democrats drawing about even in support.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  continued to push Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 message that the upcoming midterm elections were about far more than just which party controls Congress, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/edpilkington"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ed Pilkington}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  warned on Sunday that the midterm elections in November would determine whether the \u8220\'20\'1Cage-old sanctity\u8221\'20\'1D of the right to vote would be protected in the US or whether \u8220\'20\'1Cso-called extremist leaders around the country\u8221\'20\'1D would continue to restrict access to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 With just 56 days to go until the elections, and with the paper-thin Democratic majority in both chambers of Congress, the vice-president said that \u8220\'20\'1Ceverything is on the line in these elections\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with NBC News\u8217\'20\'19 Meet the Press, she said that the country was facing a rising domestic extremism threat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it is very dangerous and I think it is very harmful, and it makes us weaker,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/11/kamala-harris-midterm-elections-voting-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris says \u8216\'20\'18everything on the line\u8217\'20\'19 in midterm elections }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 The department of justice\u8217\'20\'19s investigation into the government secrets found at Mar-a-Lago ground to a halt after a Trump-appointed federal judge last week granted an order for a special master to oversee the documents.\par \par \uc2 The government is demanding that its access to the documents be restored, or it will take the matter up to a higher court. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers are expected to make their counterargument in the filing due at 10 am.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a tortuous case, and here are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/08/justice-department-appeals-special-master-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more details from The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Hugo Lowell}}}\uc2  about just what the government is asking:\par \par \uc2 The filings amounted to a forceful response against the decision by the judge \u8211\'20\'13 a Trump appointee \u8211\'20\'13 to give unusually deferential treatment to Trump on account of his status as a former president.\par \par \uc2 The justice department focused on the classified documents in its motion to stay the order barring it from reviewing the seized materials, arguing that Trump did not have \u8220\'20\'1Cpossessory interest\u8221\'20\'1D for the records \u8211\'20\'13 the key legal standard at issue \u8211\'20\'13 and were themselves the subject of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Even if Trump attempted to make an executive privilege argument to set aside the classified documents from the evidence cache, the government argued, he could not say that he had a \u8220\'20\'1Cpossessory interest\u8221\'20\'1D for classified documents that belonged to the state.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Senate intelligence committee {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/11/senate-intelligence-committee-trump-mar-a-lago-search"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wants its own briefing on what was found at Mar-a-Lago}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CSome of the documents involved human intelligence, and if that information got out people will die,\u8221\'20\'1D Mark Warner, the committee\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic chair, said on Sunday. \u8220\'20\'1CIf there were penetration of our signals intelligence, literally years of work could be destroyed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the judge\u8217\'20\'19s order also means it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear when that briefing could happen.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump to fire latest salvo in legal wrangling over Mar-a-Lago search\par \par \uc2 Good morning, US politics blog readers. Lawyers for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are up against a 10am ET deadline to submit their response to the government\u8217\'20\'19s appeal of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/05/donald-trump-special-master-fbi-mar-a-lago"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a judge\u8217\'20\'19s order}}}\uc2  allowing a special master to handle documents taken from Mar-a-Lago. The filing will be the latest \u8211\'20\'13 but certainly not the last \u8211\'20\'13 in the legal wrangling over the \uc2 FBI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s search of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Florida resort last month, and what they can do with the documents they found. Meanwhile, Congress is back in session, with Democrats looking to make the most of what may be their last few months controlling both the House and Senate ahead of November midterm elections that will decide control of the chambers.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else is happening today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is heading to Boston, where he will deliver remarks on both last year\u8217\'20\'19s infrastructure investment bill at 12.45pm, and on his administration\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgoal of ending cancer as we know it\u8221\'20\'1D at 4pm.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Congress has a ton on its plate, including a new spending agreement to keep federal agencies open, a request from the White House for $47bn for everything from Ukraine aid to Covid relief, and a bill that would head off efforts \u8211\'20\'13 mulled by Trump - to make federal employees easier to fire.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Top administration officials are in Mexico City for the US Mexico High Level Economic Dialogue between the two major trading partners, including secretary of state \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and commerce secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220912ei9c0028x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
