\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 DoJ bids to regain access to classified documents seized in Trump search \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Gloria Oladipo (now) and Richard Luscombe (earlier) \par \uc2 4,548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2022\par 20:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawyers to appeal judge\u8217\'20\'19s ruling to appoint \u8216\'20\'18special master\u8217\'20\'19Sign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it for the US politics live blog today! Here were some of the events we followed:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration announced that it plans to admit up to 125,000 refugees in next fiscal year, the same target for this current period, announced state department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gave a speech about transforming America\u8217\'20\'19s Rustbelt region into \u8220\'20\'1Cthe silicon heartland\u8221\'20\'1D while speaking at the groundbreaking of a new Intel semiconductor factory in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in Houston and spoke to astronauts aboard the international space station. The vice-president also chaired a meeting of the National Space Council this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Department of Justice lawyers aimed to regain access to highly classified documents that were seized during an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Florida mansion. Lawyers called the seized documents \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical\u8221\'20\'1D to national security.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend and thank you for reading!\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 As of August 31st, only 19,919 refugees have been admitted to the US under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 The figure falls fairly short of the 125,000 goal proposed by his administration and doesn\u8217\'20\'19t include refugees from Afghanistan and Ukraine who have come more recently.\par \par \uc2 From CNN reporter Priscilla Alvarez:\par \par \uc2 enltrThe US has admitted only 19,919 refugees into the country as of Aug 31, falling far short of the Biden admin\u8217\'20\'19s goal of 125K refugees with a month left in the fiscal year, according to federal data. That doesn't include the thousands of Afghans and Ukrainians paroled into the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Priscilla Alvarez (@priscialva) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/priscialva/status/1567889103974285314?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 US to admit up to 125,000 refugees in next fiscal year\par \par \uc2 The Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration says it will recommend to Congress a cap on refugee admissions to 125,000 for the fiscal year 2023, the same target for the current period, the state department says.\par \par \uc2 Department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.state.gov/transmission-of-the-presidents-report-to-congress-on-proposed-refugee-admissions-for-fiscal-year-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a statement}}}\uc2  that the figure would \u8220\'20\'1Caddress the growing needs generated by humanitarian crises around the globe, including the more than 100m displaced persons around the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 State department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 . Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 Under the previous administration of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the refugee cap was reduced to 15,000, its lowest ever level, something \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said during his campaign for the White House he would seek to reverse.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the past fiscal year, we have taken steps to increase the resettlement of members of particularly vulnerable populations including refugees from the Americas, Congolese, Syrians, Ukrainians, populations from Burma, and many other nationalities, as well LGBTQI+ persons, all while providing additional initial resettlement support to more than 80,000 Afghans in communities across the US,\u8221\'20\'1D Price said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US is, and will continue to be, a global leader in international humanitarian response, including through refugee resettlement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Two extremist \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters who invaded the Capitol during the 6 January riot incited by the former president pleaded guilty Friday on felony charges.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Ochs, 36, founder of the Hawaii Proud Boys chapter, and Nicholas DeCarlo, 32, a Fort Worth, Texas, resident, shared a social media channel called Murder the Media and claimed to have been in Washington DC covering the Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cstop the steal\u8221\'20\'1D rally as journalists.\par \par \uc2 They will be sentenced in December and face up to four years in prison, although the judge has discretion to go beyond the guidelines.\par \par \uc2 The men admitted to throwing smoke bombs at police trying to keep the mob from the stage set up for Biden\u8217\'20\'19s inauguration, and posed for photos in front of a door in which they scrawled \u8220\'20\'1Cmurder the media\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to the Associated Press, more than 870 people have been charged so far in the Capitol riot and almost 400 have pleaded guilty to charges ranging from low-level misdemeanors for illegally entering the building to felony seditious conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 By all appearances, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  likes to think that he represents the soul of the Maga movement. He sees himself as a tireless champion of the common man, fighting their battles against America\u8217\'20\'19s corrupt elites. It\u8217\'20\'19s not for nothing that his radio show is called War Room and carried by the Real America\u8217\'20\'19s Voice network.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . Photograph: Anadolu Agency/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 But just like everybody else who has worked closely with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  , Bannon is either delusional or trying to delude. He\u8217\'20\'19s not the everyman \u8211\'20\'13 he\u8217\'20\'19s the corrupt elite.\par \par \uc2 This was driven home once again on Thursday, when Bannon surrendered himself to New York prosecutors to face charges of defrauding donors to We Build the Wall, a non-profit organization that raised more than $25m to build a wall to keep immigrants from crossing America\u8217\'20\'19s southern border.\par \par \uc2 Although donors to the group were assured that 100% of their money would be used on construction, large sums were siphoned into the pockets of those running the group.\par \par \uc2 And who as chairman of the board allegedly took the greatest sum of all? None other than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/steve-bannon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Steve Bannon}}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Full column:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/commentisfree/2022/sep/09/steve-bannon-fraud-donald-trump-truth"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Steve Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s indictment reveals the truth about Trumpism | Andrew Gawthorpe}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth II, who died yesterday at the age of 96, visited the US as both a princess and queen, meeting more American presidents than any other head of state, according to the White House. Here\u8217\'20\'19s a pictorial look at her visits:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/gallery/2022/sep/09/the-queen-us-visits-photo-gallery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington to Yosemite: the Queen\u8217\'20\'19s visits to the US over the years \u8211\'20\'13 in pictures}}}\par \par \uc2 And for further reading, here\u8217\'20\'19s Hadley Freeman\u8217\'20\'19s look at the Queen\u8217\'20\'19s relationship with America and Americans\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Few countries are as obsessed with celebrity as the US, and royals are the ultimate celebrity, being exotically unattainable and \u8211\'20\'13 unlike most other celebrities \u8211\'20\'13 intriguingly silent. Even the most arrogant come over all awed in the Queen\u8217\'20\'19s presence. When then president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his wife, Melania, made their 2019 state visit, he might not have understood royal protocol, occasionally walking in front of the Queen during a parade, but he was still uncharacteristically respectful in her presence.\par \par \uc2 Full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/sep/09/americans-queen-elizabeth-ultimate-celebrity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 For Americans, the Queen was the ultimate celebrity | Hadley Freeman}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a taste of Joan E Greve\u8217\'20\'19s report on another (and more explicitly political) Biden speech, made in Maryland on Thursday night\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  continued his attacks on \u8220\'20\'1C extreme Maga Republicans \u8221\'20\'1D on Thursday night, as he spoke at the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  summer meeting in Oxon Hill, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in a serious moment in this nation\u8217\'20\'19s history,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why those who love this country \u8211\'20\'13 Democrats, independents and mainstream Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 have to be stronger, more determined and more committed to saving American democracy than the Maga Republicans are to literally destroying American politics. You just have to vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Full report:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/09/biden-motivate-voters-maga-republicans-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden seeks to motivate voters from all parties against \u8216\'20\'18Maga Republicans destroying politics\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden: US will 'lead world' in industries of the future\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says it\u8217\'20\'19s time to \u8220\'20\'1Cbury the label Rust Belt and call it the silicon heartland\u8221\'20\'1D as he touts the groundbreaking of a new Intel semiconductor factory in Ohio, and what he says is a new dawn for US technology.\par \par \uc2 The president is taking a victory tour of the US following the passage of the $52bn bipartisan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2022/09/09/semiconductor-chips-act-intel-panasonic-plants/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chips and Science Act}}}\uc2  this summer that he hopes will kickstart the stalled production of semiconductors in the US.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  attends the groundbreaking of the new Intel semiconductor manufacturing facility in New Albany, Ohio. Photograph: Joshua Roberts/Reuters\par \par \uc2 That shortfall, he says, has held back the country\u8217\'20\'19s automobile, healthcare, manufacturing and other industries:\par \par \uc2 The US has to lead the world in producing advanced chips, and this law makes sure that we will.\par \par \uc2 The act moves us up once again. We\u8217\'20\'19re going to make sure we lead the world in industries of the future, in quantum computing, artificial intelligence, advanced biotechnology\u8230\'20\'26 think of the things this kind of investment can deliver, vaccines for cancer, cures for HIV, inventing the next best thing that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t even been imagined yet.\par \par \uc2 Aware of criticism from some Republicans, and others, that the Chips Act is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/26/us-chips-act-intel-robert-reich"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 corporate giveaway}}}\uc2 , and threatens national security by sending taxpayer money to companies who do business with global competitors including China, Biden made an attempt at reassurance:\par \par \uc2 The act is not handing out blank checks to companies. I\u8217\'20\'19ve directed my administration to be laser focused on the guardrails that will protect taxpayers dollars.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll make sure that companies partner with unions, community colleges, technical schools, to offer training and apprenticeships and to work with small minority owned businesses.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re going to make sure that companies that take taxpayer dollars don\u8217\'20\'19t turn around and make investments in China to undermine our supply chain and national security. We have the power to take back any federal funding if companies don\u8217\'20\'19t meet these requirements.\par \par \uc2 The Intel factory, Biden says, is a \u8220\'20\'1Cfield of dreams\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s fitting to break ground for American infrastructure here in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Think about our tradition here. The Wright brothers, Neil Armstrong, John Glenn... they defined American spirit, a sprit of daring innovation.\par \par \uc2 Intel\u8217\'20\'19s vision builds on that legacy, a brand new $20bn campus, 7,000 construction jobs, 3,000 full-time jobs paying an average of $135,000 a year and not all of them require a college degree.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s finished the speech now, which was focused entirely on the Chips Act and did not veer off into any other global events.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in the intriguingly named Licking county, Ohio, where he\u8217\'20\'19s at the groundbreaking ceremony of a new Intel factory and about to deliver remarks about the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2022/09/09/semiconductor-chips-act-intel-panasonic-plants/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chips Act}}}\uc2 , which will boost US production of semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 The White House says this particular groundbreaking is a different occasion from the usual \u8220\'20\'1Cscripted, backslapping affairs for political dignitaries\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re about to find out, but so far various industry officials and political dignitaries have spent a lot of time lauding each other and talking about the forthcoming factory.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you the best of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s remarks when he delivers them.\par \par \uc2 enltr"Groundbreaking ceremonies are often scripted, backslapping affairs for political dignitaries, but this marks an important milestone for a project with major regional and national implications." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dCFJNsQkVC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/dCFJNsQkVC}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Herbie Ziskend (@HerbieZiskend46) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HerbieZiskend46/status/1568271751800307714?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Self-confessed \u8220\'20\'1Cspace nerd\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had a problem in Houston this morning as she spoke with astronauts aboard the international space station.\par \par \uc2 The vice-president is at the Johnson Space Center to chair an afternoon visit of the National Space Council, and took the opportunity to call astronauts Jessica Watkins, Bob Hines and Kjell Lindgren orbiting 250 miles above Earth. It didn\u8217\'20\'19t end well.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe National Space Council, chaired by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/VP?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @VP}}}\uc2 , will meet today at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NASA_Johnson?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NASA_Johnson}}}\uc2 . The vice president will talk to astronauts aboard the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Space_Station?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Space_Station}}}\uc2  before sharing remarks about advancing space exploration & touring the home of the astronaut corps. Details: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/A5wHAyGz6M"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/A5wHAyGz6M}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/LGpd8wP3IF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/LGpd8wP3IF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 NASA\uc2  (@NASA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NASA/status/1568217325706551304?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Harris and the ISS crew exchanged pleasantries and a conversation about plants being grown on the space station, and the astronauts\u8217\'20\'19 perspective looking down on the planet.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe look down and we see a world with no borders,\u8221\'20\'1D Hines said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou realize how fragile it is and how much we have to take care of it as well\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The trouble came when Harris, who introduced herself to the crew as a \u8220\'20\'1Cspace nerd\u8221\'20\'1D, wanted some guidance for young people who might want to become astronauts, or part of the \uc2 Nasa\uc2  team supporting their missions. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s your advice for our students?\u8221\'20\'1D she wondered.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I think that\u8230\'20\'26\u8221\'20\'1D the reply came, followed by the audible equivalent of a black hole.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust as the vice-president was asking her question, we passed out a range of our tracking and data relay satellite system,\u8221\'20\'1D a \uc2 Nasa\uc2  commentator on the ground interjected.\par \par \uc2 Harris will deliver remarks this afternoon about \u8220\'20\'1Cadvancing space exploration\u8221\'20\'1D, six days after \uc2 Nasa\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s most recent attempt to launch its new Artemis 1 moon rocket from Florida was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2022/sep/03/nasa-artemis-1-launch-called-off-fuel-leak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called off for technical problems}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 There are developments in the war in Ukraine, with Russian forces being driven out of areas they formerly occupied and the Ukrainian military appearing to be closing in on the city of Kupiansk, a key logistical hub for the invading forces in the Kharkiv region.\par \par \uc2 The US, with its western allies, continues to provide military and humanitarian support for the Ukrainian defense effort, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announcing two weeks ago the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/aug/24/joe-biden-ukraine-military-aid-zelenskiy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 largest tranche of aid to date}}}\uc2 , bringing to $13bn the total the country has supplied or pledged to Kyiv since the president took office.\par \par \uc2 A reminder that you can follow all the developments in the Ukraine-Russia war in our live blog here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/sep/09/ukraine-russia-war-live-zelenskiy-counteroffensive-pressure-russian-troops"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ukraine-Russia war live: nuclear watchdog calls for Zaporizhzhia safety zone to avoid accident}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 On the night of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/nov/07/democratic-presidential-candidates-2020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2018 midterm elections}}}\uc2 , as a wave of anti-Trump sentiment swept Democrats to take control of the House, top Republican \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  urged \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run for president.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to run,\u8221\'20\'1D said Romney, the Republican presidential nominee Biden and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  defeated in 2012, speaking to the former vice-president by phone.\par \par \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 . Photograph: Elizabeth Frantz/Reuters\par \par \uc2 The same night, Romney was elected a US senator from Utah, a post from which he would twice vote to convict {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  in impeachment trials.\par \par \uc2 Romney\u8217\'20\'19s exhortation to a man then seen as a likely challenger to Trump in 2020 will probably further enrage the former president, his supporters and the Republican party they dominate.\par \par \uc2 The Biden-Romney call is described in The Long Alliance: The Imperfect Union of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , a book by Gabriel Debenedetti that will be published next week. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  obtained a copy.\par \par \uc2 Describing how Biden spent 6 November 2018, Debenedetti writes: \u8220\'20\'1CBiden spent election night glued to his phone as usual \u8230\'20\'26 He talked to most of the candidates he\u8217\'20\'19d campaigned for, and plenty he didn\u8217\'20\'19t, either to congratulate or console them, or just to catch up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis time felt better than 2016\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 when Trump beat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  for the presidency \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cin part because {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  were winning big, at least in local races and in the House.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut it was also because of a refrain [Biden] kept hearing, and not always from the most expected sources.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt one point he connected with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/mittromney"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mitt Romney}}}\uc2 , who\u8217\'20\'19d been easily elected to the Senate that night as a rare Trump-opposing Republican. They were warm as Biden cheered Romney\u8217\'20\'19s win.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThen Obama\u8217\'20\'19s old rival got to the point: You have to run, Romney said.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2022/sep/09/mitt-romney-joe-biden-trump-book-obama-debenedetti"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18You have to run\u8217\'20\'19: Romney urged Biden to take down Trump, book says}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Florida has dismissed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit against 2016 Democratic rival \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and former top \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, rejecting the former president\u8217\'20\'19s claims that they and others acted in concert to concoct the Russia investigation that shadowed much of his administration.\par \par \uc2 According to the Associated Press, US district judge Donald Middlebrooks said in a sharply worded ruling that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit, filed in March, contained \u8220\'20\'1Cglaring structural deficiencies\u8221\'20\'1D and that many of the \u8220\'20\'1Ccharacterizations of events are implausible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 James Comey\uc2 . Photograph: J Scott Applewhite/AP\par \par \uc2 He dismissed the idea that Trump had sued to correct an actual legal harm, saying that \u8220\'20\'1Cinstead, he is seeking to flaunt a 200-page political manifesto outlining his grievances against those that have opposed him, and this court is not the appropriate forum.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit named as defendants Clinton and some of her top advisers, as well as former \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  and other \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials involved in the investigation into whether Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2016 presidential campaign had coordinated with Russia to sway the outcome of the election.\par \par \uc2 A 2019 justice department inspector general report identified flaws by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  during the Russia investigation, but did not find evidence that the bureau\u8217\'20\'19s leaders were motivated by political bias.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ginni Thomas, the self-styled \u8220\'20\'1Cculture warrior\u8221\'20\'1D and extreme rightwing activist, has links to more than half of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and individuals who lobbied her husband \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and his fellow {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court}}}\uc2  justices ahead of their historic decision to eradicate a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A new analysis of the written legal arguments, or \u8220\'20\'1Camicus briefs\u8221\'20\'1D, used to lobby the justices as they deliberated over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  underlines the extent to which \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s wife was intertwined with this vast pressure campaign.\par \par \uc2 Ginni Thomas. Photograph: Patrick Semansky/AP\par \par \uc2 The survey found that 51% of the parties who filed amicus briefs calling for an end to a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right have political connections to Ginni Thomas, raising concerns about a possible conflict of interest at the highest levels of the US judiciary.\par \par \uc2 The six-to-three rightwing majority of the court, supercharged by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s three appointed conservative justices, in June overthrew the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was among the six who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted for the hotly contested ruling}}}\uc2 , Dobbs v Jackson.\par \par \uc2 The ruling was one of the most consequential in the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s 233-year history. It has triggered the lightning spread of partial or total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans across Republican-controlled states, affecting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/25/us-abortion-trigger-bans-laws-tennessee-texas-idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost one in three women}}}\uc2  aged 15 to 44.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs case, brought by Mississippi which sought to ban abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, attracted an almost unprecedented 130 amicus briefs from both sides of the legal argument. Of those, 74 were filed in favour of overturning the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , enshrined in 1973 in Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 In turn, the new analysis shows that 38 of the 74 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amicus briefs \u8211\'20\'13 51% \u8211\'20\'13 were produced by entities and individuals with links to Ginni Thomas. They included rightwing groups, religious interests, prominent conservative individuals and lawyers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Thomases are normalizing the prospect of too close an association between the supreme court and those who litigate before it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Melissa Murray, a law professor at \uc2 New York University\uc2  and co-host of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://strict-scrutiny.simplecast.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Strict Scrutiny podcast}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t the first time that Mrs Thomas has had dealings with those who come before the court and seek her husband\u8217\'20\'19s vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/09/ginni-thomas-abortion-roe-v-wade-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Revealed: Ginni Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s links to anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  groups who lobbied to overturn Roe}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Washington Post\u8217\'20\'19s White House reporter Matt Viser has taken a fascinating look at some of the similarities between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and King Charles III.\par \par \uc2 The White House said Friday that Biden would join other world leaders in attending the state funeral in London of Charles\u8217\'20\'19s mother, Queen Elizabeth II, on a date yet to be confirmed, but likely to be Monday 19 September.\par \par \uc2 enltrBiden was the 13th US president to meet with Queen Elizabeth II \u8212\'20\'14 and will likely now be the first to meet with King Charles III. Both men late in life assumed a role they spent decades hoping for, bringing experience after having served as an understudy. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/8qIJrBWCRw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/8qIJrBWCRw}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Matt Viser (@mviser) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mviser/status/1568210964415119360?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Viser notes that Biden was the 13th US president to meet the Queen, and will likely become the first to meet with King Charles after his accession.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBoth are men who late in life assumed a role they had spent decades positioning themselves for, and who took their positions with a deep well of experience after having served as an understudy. They also arguably capture less of the public\u8217\'20\'19s fascination than their predecessors,\u8221\'20\'1D he writes.\par \par \uc2 You can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/09/09/biden-queen-elizabeth-mother/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 read the article here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 When the far-right firebrand {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/steve-bannon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Steve Bannon}}}\uc2  was hit with fresh {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/07/steve-bannon-to-be-indicted-on-fresh-charges-over-border-wall-sources"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fraud charges}}}\uc2  for an alleged border wall fundraising scheme, he joined the ranks of several close \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cronies recently prosecuted by the Manhattan district attorney\u8217\'20\'19s office.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, then district attorney \uc2 Cyrus Vance Jr\uc2  brought mortgage fraud charges against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/paul-manafort"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Paul Manafort}}}\uc2  , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2016 campaign chairman. And in 2021, the current district attorney, Alvin Bragg, charged the former \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO Allen Weisselberg with fraud, and Ken Kurson with felony cyberstalking, in separate cases.\par \par \uc2 Alvin Bragg. Photograph: Alex Kent/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Put together the cases suggest that the actions of some top Trump allies can still generate legal headaches long after Trump left the White House and also despite being issued pardons.\par \par \uc2 Charges do not necessarily lead to convictions, of course, let alone hard prison time, as evidenced by the outcome of these past cases. Manafort, who was convicted in federal court before the New York case unfolded, ultimately didn\u8217\'20\'19t face state charges on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/08/paul-manafort-mortgage-fraud-charges-new-york-double-jeopardy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 double-jeopardy grounds}}}\uc2 . Manafort was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/dec/23/donald-trumps-latest-wave-of-pardons-includes-paul-manafort-and-charles-kunsher"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pardoned}}}\uc2  by Trump about two months before the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/feb/08/paul-manafort-mortgage-fraud-charges-new-york-double-jeopardy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision}}}\uc2  came down that he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be tried in state court due to double jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Kurson \u8211\'20\'13 who was first charged in Brooklyn federal court for cyberstalking, but pardoned by Trump before he left office \u8211\'20\'13 pleaded guilty to two misdemeanors and was ordered to do community service in his state case. Weisselberg, meanwhile, is expected to serve just 100 days in local jail under his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/18/allen-weisselberg-trump-organization-pleads-guilty-tax-fraud"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plea deal}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the surface, some might wonder whether Bannon\u8217\'20\'19s state-level case has the same legal weakness as Manafort\u8217\'20\'19s did. Bannon was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/aug/20/steve-bannon-arrested-charged-fraud-we-build-the-wall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charged}}}\uc2  federally in August 2020 for allegedly siphoning more than $1m from the \u8220\'20\'1CWe Build the Wall\u8221\'20\'1D online fundraising campaign. Trump also pardoned Bannon before his case went to trial.\par \par \uc2 But longtime attorneys told the Guardian that Bragg\u8217\'20\'19s Bannon case was different from Vance\u8217\'20\'19s Manafort prosecution because when Bannon was pardoned, state-level charges for the same alleged misconduct do not carry the same double-jeopardy risks, they said.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/09/bannon-trump-allies-new-york-legal-issues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Legal fallout for Trump cronies persists despite his pardons}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Justice department says documents 'critical' to national security\par \par \uc2 The justice department\u8217\'20\'19s legal filing is expected sometime today expanding its arguments why district court judge Aileen Cannon, a Trump appointee, must reverse her decision appointing a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial master\u8221\'20\'1D in the case of the former president\u8217\'20\'19s hoarding of classified materials at his Florida residence.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/08/justice-department-appeals-special-master-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strongly-worded notice of intent}}}\uc2  to appeal submitted on Thursday, department lawyers let Cannon know in no uncertain terms that her decision was impeding the progress of an investigation critical to national security.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Photograph: Mandel Ngan/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 The lawyers made clear it needed access back immediately to classified documents seized last month at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Mar-a-Lago mansion by \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, while Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys are claiming he is entitled to have sent back to him everything that was taken away.\par \par \uc2 The inquiry took on added poignancy this week when it was reported that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/06/donald-trump-mar-a-lago-documents-nuclear-weapons-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another country\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear secrets}}}\uc2  were among the stash of highly classified documents Trump is said to have hidden from federal agents.\par \par \uc2 The department will make its own arguments, but we couldn\u8217\'20\'19t explain things any better than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/playbook"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico Playbook likening Trump to a jewel thief}}}\uc2  demanding the return of his ill-gotten gains:\par \par \uc2 Imagine that someone allegedly stole a sack of diamonds from a jewelry shop and then stashed the gems in junk drawers around their house. The cops raid the place, take away everything in the drawers where they find stolen diamonds, and spend two weeks separating them from the junk.\par \par \uc2 Then a judge comes along and says that the big issue in the case isn\u8217\'20\'19t the stolen diamonds but that the cops still have some of the alleged thief\u8217\'20\'19s personal belongings. She halts the heist investigation until an outside expert can sort the gems from the junk.\par \par \uc2 The government thinks the judge\u8217\'20\'19s decision is absurd - no other suspect has received this special treatment - but they offer the judge a compromise: let us keep all of the diamonds, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll return all of the alleged thief\u8217\'20\'19s junk, even a few cheap watches that they think he might have swiped from the store.\par \par \uc2 Also today, the justice department and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s legal team were due to jointly file a list of possible candidates to serve as the \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial master\u8221\'20\'1D to review the records seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you news on both fronts as we get it.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/09/trump-special-master-candidates-mar-a-lago-search"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump lawyers and justice department to file list of special master candidates}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 DoJ lawyers aim to regain access of Trump files\par \par \uc2 Good morning, it\u8217\'20\'19s Friday, and welcome to our US politics blog.\par \par \uc2 An unusually busy week has plenty more to offer, including the justice department spelling out today in a legal filing its arguments for regaining access to highly classified documents seized in an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Florida mansion.\par \par \uc2 Department lawyers on Thursday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/08/justice-department-appeals-special-master-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said they would appeal the ruling}}}\uc2  by district judge Aileen Cannon to appoint a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial master\u8221\'20\'1D in its investigation of the former president\u8217\'20\'19s improper hoarding of confidential materials \u8211\'20\'13 including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/sep/07/trump-nuclear-document-special-master-biden-obama-bannon-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another nation\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear secrets}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 at his residence, arguing her decision was blocking an inquiry critical to national security.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ll flesh out their arguments in today\u8217\'20\'19s expected legal filing, and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers will have until Monday to respond. But it\u8217\'20\'19s already evident the justice department is playing hardball. We\u8217\'20\'19ll have more analysis coming up.\par \par \uc2 The White House, meanwhile, announced that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would join other world leaders at the state funeral of Queen Elizabeth II in London, expected to be on Monday 19 September.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else we\u8217\'20\'19re watching today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is heading for Ohio and the groundbreaking for a new Intel factory, where he\u8217\'20\'19ll deliver remarks on the Chips Act at 12.15pm.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s no scheduled White House media briefing, but press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  will hold a \u8220\'20\'1Cgaggle\u8221\'20\'1D for reporters aboard Air Force One en route to Ohio.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is in Houston to talk to astronauts aboard the international space station, and chair a meeting of the National Space Council this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a day off and a long weekend for both the US House and Senate, so we\u8217\'20\'19re not expecting big news out of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220909ei99001xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
