\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans help Democratic minority block near-total  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban in South Carolina\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Luscombe and agencies \par \uc2 398 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2022\par 13:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Opposition by quintet means Republicans lack votes in upper chamber to end filibuster, with bill heading back to state house\par \par \uc2 Five Republicans in South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s state senate have joined the Democratic minority to reject a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, thwarting the controlling party\u8217\'20\'19s intention to capitalize on the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn federal protections for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Their opposition at a special session called on Thursday to approve a restrictive bill passed earlier in the South Carolina House meant Republicans lacked enough votes in the upper chamber, which they control 30-16, to end a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 State senate majority leader Shane Massey called a recess to discuss options after it became clear the near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal could not pass, and Republicans had to settle instead on a number of tweaks to the state\u8217\'20\'19s existing six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 They include cutting the time that pregnant victims of rape and incest have to seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 20 weeks to about 12 weeks, and requiring that DNA from an aborted fetus is retained for law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The bill now heads back to the house, which passed a total ban with exceptions only for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The statewide six-week ban, meanwhile, is currently suspended while the South Carolina supreme court weighs whether it breaches privacy laws.\par \par \uc2 One of the most vocal Republican opponents to the measure was Tom Davis, ex-chief of staff to former governor \uc2 Mark Sanford\uc2 , who was joined by three female and one male colleague.\par \par \uc2 Davis said he promised his daughters he would not vote to make restrictions even tighter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe moment we become pregnant we lose all control over what goes on with our bodies,\u8221\'20\'1D Davis said, recalling what his daughters told him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m here to tell you I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to let it happen.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And in Michigan, the state\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court ruled on Thursday that voters will decide in November if the constitution will be amended to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The move was a victory for pro-choice advocates who wanted the option on midterms ballot papers.\par \par \uc2 In deeply conservative Kansas last month, voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chose overwhelmingly to protect \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 , fueling Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 hope for a sweeping nationwide backlash to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Roe v Wade ruling.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220909ei990020a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
