\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Could unexpected Democratic gains foil a midterm Republican victory?\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino and David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2022\par 16:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A string of favorable outcomes for the party, coupled with backlash from supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, could galvanize Biden supporters\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  travels to the battleground states of Wisconsin and Pennsylvania on Monday, determined to reframe America\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections as a defining choice between democracy and the extremism of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fighting for every vote, the US president will mark Labor Day with growing confidence as opinion polls suggest that, while Republicans still have the lead, Democrats now have the momentum heading into the home stretch.\par \par \uc2 A string of unlikely legislative victories, combined with fury at the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could galvanize Biden\u8217\'20\'19s supporters to turn out in huge numbers in November.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, for their part, have suffered self-inflicted wounds. Several of their Senate candidates \u8211\'20\'13 such as football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia \u8211\'20\'13 have celebrity but little political pedigree. The prospect of Trump facing criminal prosecution for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/02/trump-fbi-search-materials-list-fbi-latest?CMP=twt_b-gdnnews&utm_medium=Social&utm_source=Twitter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 keeping top secret government papers}}}\uc2  at his Mar-a-Lago home in Florida has also cast a long shadow.\par \par \uc2 The midterms are typically a referendum on the incumbent president and his party usually loses seats. But recent events have left Republicans rattled and Democrats dreaming of not only holding the Senate but \u8211\'20\'13 unthinkable just a few months ago \u8211\'20\'13 retaining their majority in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe good news for us heading into Labor Day is we\u8217\'20\'19re on offense,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TomPerez?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tom Perez}}}\uc2 , who was chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee (DNC) \uc2 during the 2018 midterm elections. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re putting points on the board and we\u8217\'20\'19re building momentum. And politics, especially in midterm politics, is all about momentum.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Neither Biden nor Trump is on the ballot but both loom large in the midterms, offering {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/01/us/politics/biden-trump.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a replay of the 2020 election}}}\uc2  and preview of a possible 2024 rematch.\par \par \uc2 On Monday the current president will speak at Milwaukee\u8217\'20\'19s Laborfest celebration in Wisconsin, where incumbent Ron Johnson, a Trump ally, is the most vulnerable Republican senator up for re-election this cycle.\par \par \uc2 Biden will also stop in Pittsburgh, host to one of the oldest and biggest Labor Day parades, marking his third visit to Pennsylvania in the space of a week. Democrats \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  are battling TV doctor Mehmet Oz and election denier {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/20/doug-mastriano-pennsylvania-republican-governor-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Doug Mastriano}}}\uc2  for the Senate and governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion respectively. Eighteen House seats are also up for grabs in the state.\par \par \uc2 In remarks in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, on Thursday night, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/01/joe-biden-speech-democracy-republican-extremism-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden described \u8220\'20\'1CMaga Republicans\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 using the acronym for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CMake America great again\u8221\'20\'1D campaign slogan \u8211\'20\'13 as representing an extremism that threatens the foundations of the American republic. This followed previous comments in which he likened the Maga philosophy to \u8220\'20\'1Csemi-fascism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lakeresearch.com/celinda-lake"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Celinda Lake}}}\uc2 , a veteran Democratic pollster who worked for Biden\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 presidential campaign, said such hardening rhetoric is helping to recast the election as a high-stakes contest between the president\u8217\'20\'19s agenda and a return to Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 The idea that if you want to beat Trump in 2024 you have to beat him in 2022 is very salient to Democrats\par \par \uc2 Celinda Lake\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt makes the return of Trump and Trump Republicans very frightening to people,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd the idea that if you want to beat Trump in 2024 you have to beat him in 2022 is very salient to Democrats.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For months, Democrats have been urging Biden to use the full force of his bully pulpit. In focus groups, Lake said, one of the major critiques she hears of Democrats is not that they are too liberal, but that they are \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo weak\u8221\'20\'1D. Asked to name an animal that describes each party, participants often choose turtle or sloth for Democrats and wolverine or bull for Republicans, she said.\par \par \uc2 Lake added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to show strength and I think the president is really doing that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden also used his prime time address to make something of a campaign speech, touting a record that includes \u8220\'20\'1Cthe largest economic recovery package since Franklin Delano Roosevelt \u8230\'20\'26 the biggest infrastructure investment since President \uc2 Dwight D Eisenhower\uc2  \u8230\'20\'26 the most significant gun safety law since President Clinton \u8230\'20\'26 the most significant healthcare reforms since President \uc2 Obama\uc2  signed the Affordable Care Act \u8230\'20\'26 the most important climate initiative ever\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 These victories, despite wafer-thin majorities in Congress, along with rising job numbers and falling gas prices, have given Democrats renewed hope on the campaign trail. But perhaps the biggest gamechanger was the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the 1973 ruling that recognised a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has a 6-3 conservative majority including three justices appointed by Trump. The political backlash became clear when, in conservative Kansas, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters decisively rejected}}}\uc2  a constitutional amendment that would have nullified a state supreme court decision guaranteeing the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats are seizing on the issue, stressing that reproductive rights will be on the ballot in November. Republicans have been forced on the defensive, with several candidates in swing states removing hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language from their campaign websites.\par \par \uc2 Lake called the supreme court decision a \u8220\'20\'1Cclass 9 earthquake\u8221\'20\'1D that has profoundly reshaped the political landscape in ways Republicans failed to foresee.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.american.edu/cas/faculty/lichtman.cfm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Allan Lichtman}}}\uc2 , a history professor at \uc2 American University in Washington\uc2 , agreed that it has \u8220\'20\'1Ctectonically shifted the plates of American politics \u8221\'20\'1D, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you look at the losses in 2018, 2014, 2010, they were more typical midterm losses for the party holding the White House. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t have this kind of burning issue that affected people\u8217\'20\'19s daily lives the way the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans with no exception for rape or incest are just one example, in Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 telling, of how extreme \u8220\'20\'1CMaga Republicans\u8221\'20\'1D have become. Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 effort to make the election about Trump, rather than inflation or crime or border security, has received several assists from the former president, who is embroiled in legal battles, holding rallies and dominating headlines as of old.\par \par \uc2 As the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/09/01/trump-midterms-gop-mcconnell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Axios website put it}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CGOP midterm candidates \u8211\'20\'13 who want to talk solely about the prices of gas and groceries \u8211\'20\'13 now must contend with background music that once again is Trump, Trump, Trump.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Perez, now co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century, a Democratic super Pac, said:\u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t just base Democrats coming together, it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of independent voters who are being reminded on a daily basis why they didn\u8217\'20\'19t vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Perez pointed to Oz in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  in Arizona and JD Vance in Ohio as far-right Trump-backed nominees who are motivating Democrats and repelling independents. \u8220\'20\'1CA secondary force that is giving us wind at our back is the fact that the other side is not only extreme, but they are fielding extreme candidates.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans, including the Senate minority leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , share the concerns. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/ed-rogers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ed Rogers}}}\uc2 , a political consultant who worked in the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 George H W Bush\uc2  administrations, said: \u8220\'20\'1CAt the top of the ticket, the governor and the senator in Pennsylvania worry me.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Arizona, I\u8217\'20\'19m worried that [Democratic incumbent] {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://markkelly.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mark Kelly}}}\uc2  is not a good villain and we\u8217\'20\'19ve got a kind of wacky guy, I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid \u8211\'20\'13 he needs to pull out of some wackiness. But what may save us is the border issue. There is zero evidence of anyone listening to Mark Kelly about any moderation at the border.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of independent voters who are being reminded on a daily basis why they didn\u8217\'20\'19t vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 Tom Perez\par \par \uc2 Even so, Rogers remains confident that a continuation of the 50-50 split in the Senate is the \u8220\'20\'1Cworst-case scenario\u8221\'20\'1D for Republicans, and that they will \u8220\'20\'1Ccertainly\u8221\'20\'1D win the House. He added: \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody\u8217\'20\'19s chasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rabbit and incrementally, that\u8217\'20\'19ll be better for the Democrats. Everybody\u8217\'20\'19s chasing the search warrant down at Mar-a-Lago and this and that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CForget all that. Keep your eyes focused on the big four: inflation and the economy; crime and the breakdown of social order, particularly in our cities; the border and immigration out of control; and the breakdown of public education in a lot of jurisdictions of America. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The election could prove less of a referendum on Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/newsletters/axios-am-d5139934-84f6-42ee-ba26-e6243fe7e6dd.html?chunk=0&utm_term=emshare#story0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 than on Trump}}}\uc2 . Should his favoured candidates lose in Arizona, Pennsylvania and elsewhere, and should moderate Republican nominees succeed in states such as New Mexico and Oregon, there is likely to be renewed debate over his influence in the party.\par \par \uc2 Democrats received more positive omens this week. The party\u8217\'20\'19s Mary Peltola won in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/09/01/democrat-mary-peltola-wins-upset-in-alaska-special-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 special election}}}\uc2  over Trump-backed Republican \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  for the House seat in Alaska. The widely respected Cook Political Report, a non-partisan newsletter, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/analysis/house/house-overview/house-rating-changes-five-races-move-democrats-direction-including"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moved the ratings}}}\uc2  of five House seats in Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 direction.\par \par \uc2 But given that Democrats have a narrow 221-214 majority in the House, Republicans only need to pick up four seats and, the Cook Political Report estimates, they are likely to pick up roughly three automatically thanks to the redrawing of congressional district boundaries.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://watson.brown.edu/people/faculty-fellows/schiller"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wendy Schiller,}}}\uc2  a political science professor at \uc2 Brown University\uc2  in Providence, Rhode Island, said: \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans still go into the midterms with an advantage in House districts, which are gerrymandered to favor one party or the other, and voters always like the opportunity to register their dissatisfaction with the party in power in a midterm election.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the Senate, the upper midwest states of Wisconsin, Ohio and Pennsylvania are the real indicators of party strength nationally. In each of these states, the Senate GOP candidate has high negatives but the GOP has made strong inroads among voters who are not happy with Biden, and anger is a good motivator to get out the vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is also unclear what turnout rates will be among African American voters this midterm and, without a strong turnout among that voting base, the Democrats will have a difficult time winning there, and in other states like Georgia and North Carolina.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The midterms will determine control of Congress for the second half of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency. Democratic majorities could allow him to press on with an ambitious agenda; Republican majorities could effectively turn him into a lame duck. Either scenario will have an impact on the presidential race in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Lichtman of American University believes that Biden will emerge strengthened. \u8220\'20\'1CNo matter what happens, expectations are everything and Republicans are not going to live up to expectations,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think they\u8217\'20\'19ll perform as expected in a midterm election and it\u8217\'20\'19s likely that Democrats hold the Senate, which is vastly more important than the House for the simple reason that they confirm all the judges. Biden has a lot of judicial appointments to make to counter the many that Trump did. So if they lose the Senate, it would be a disaster because you would imagine \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s going to block everything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220905ei95000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
