\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights movement can learn from the Aids activism of the 80s and 90s\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,231 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2022\par 10:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Act Up employed a multiplicity of tactics and responses while maintaining a laser-focused singleness of purpose\par \par \uc2 It was already chaos, and now, it was going to get worse. The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision wasn\u8217\'20\'19t exactly a surprise \u8211\'20\'13 everyone knew it was coming \u8211\'20\'13 but the rage in the room felt palpable. The language of the opinion had been taunting and cruel; the punishment from the court felt vindictive, personal. People were already dying for lack of access to healthcare; the ruling would push people in need even further to the margins. Now, the most vulnerable faced criminalization, harassment and even death, and for what? For the conservative Christian values that none of them had voted for? For a regressive, punitive, and cruel vision of gender and sexuality that most of the world had long since left behind?\par \par \uc2 The ruling was Bowers v Hardwick, and the year was 1987. The supreme court, in a virulently homophobic opinion, had upheld a Georgia law criminalizing gay sex between consenting adults. At the time, the Aids crisis was gripping gay America. Out of bigotry and indifference, both the federal government and the pharmaceutical sector were dragging their feet. Meanwhile, thousands of people, mostly gay men and IV drug users, were dying slow, painful, premature deaths, at the margins of a society that hated them and feared their disease.\par \par \uc2 The moral stakes couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been more clear: a backwards and oppressive understanding of gender was creating needless suffering and death. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the birth of Act Up, the radical grassroots activist group that confronted the Aids crisis in America, but it was the moment when Act Up was energized into the powerful force it would become. At the group\u8217\'20\'19s regular Monday night meetings at New York\u8217\'20\'19s Lesbian and Gay Center, the crowd swelled with outraged queers ready to be organized.\par \par \uc2 The reproductive rights movement now faces a similar moment of rage and revival. Since the Dobbs opinion reversed Roe v Wade and eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there has been an outpouring of pro-choice sentiment from previously unorganized or apolitical citizens. Meanwhile, an onslaught of horror stories is pouring out from conservative states: miscarrying patients going into septic shock before they can be treated; women forced to carry dead or doomed fetuses for weeks; little girls, raped and impregnated by men they thought they could trust, fleeing their states to get the abortions that will allow them to reclaim what\u8217\'20\'19s left of their childhoods. This is just the beginning: there will be more of these stories, many more, and they will get worse.\par \par \uc2 Like the gay community in 1987, American women are now faced with a moment of profound terror, anger, and grief whose full extent is yet to be seen. A slogan advanced in recent years by the pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group We Testify attempts to destigmatize the procedure: everyone loves someone who\u8217\'20\'19s had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Soon, everyone will know someone who has needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and struggled to get it.\par \par \uc2 Moments of feminist rage aren\u8217\'20\'19t uncommon in American politics, but the ability to harness women\u8217\'20\'19s anger for political ends has been a trickier feat, especially in recent years. The Women\u8217\'20\'19s March, which followed Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election, drew in giant numbers for street protests, but was unable to harness its support toward a specific agenda. The organization was hobbled by infighting and lack of direction. Later, the #MeToo movement was able to generate public conversation and remove a number of high-profile abusers from positions of power. But #MeToo was not able to translate its moral authority into a political platform.\par \par \uc2 Act Up offers a different model, one with a proven record of success. Like feminists and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters now, Act Up was composed of people of varying backgrounds, commitments, and ideologies. They had different priorities, skills, and ideas; different perspectives and styles. But they all shared the same goal: to combat the Aids crisis, and to improve the lives of people with Aids.\par \par \uc2 The writer and teacher Sarah Schulman, an Act Up veteran, attributes the group\u8217\'20\'19s success to \u8220\'20\'1Ca strategy of difference facilitating simultaneity of response\u8221\'20\'1D. In other words, a lot of different kinds of people were using different tactics, all in pursuit of the same things. Under this broad but well-defined agenda, the group was able to transform its varied constituency into an asset \u8211\'20\'13 not a liability.\par \par \uc2 Over the five years that it was most active and influential, 1987-1992, the group was able to successfully lobby to lower drug prices, get more people included in trials, extend Aids benefits to women, and cut red tape to get more treatments to market. Their loud, aggressive, and irreverent public demonstrations at the \uc2 FDA\uc2  building and in the offices of pharmaceutical executives were carefully targeted and publicized, meant to garner publicity and also to be as uncomfortable and inconvenient as possible to the powerful people who were standing in their way. Now, the mainstream media scolds protesters for holding demure vigils outside the home of \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in Chevy Chase, Maryland. Back then, Act Up went down to DC to protest outside a politician\u8217\'20\'19s home, too. They found out the address of the rightwing, anti-gay North Carolina senator Jesse Helms, and with the news cameras rolling, put a giant condom over his house.\par \par \uc2 Maybe one of the great lessons of Act Up is this willingness to embrace irreverence and joy, something the reproductive rights movement, to their great credit, have also embraced. But another virtue is that the group employed a multiplicity of tactics and responses while maintaining a singleness of purpose.\par \par \uc2 Feminism, as a movement, has long had a problem of unsustainably expanding responsibilities. Because women exist in all walks of life, any social problem can be cast as a feminist problem. But no movement can take on responsibility for every injustice in the world. Like Act Up, the reproductive rights movement would be wise to assign itself an expansive understanding of a narrow remit.\par \par \uc2 Act Up aimed to combat the Aids crisis and improve the lives of people with Aids, a purview that enabled different factions within the group to take on issues of drug access, housing discrimination, sex education, and the power of the Catholic church. Likewise, the reproductive rights movement would be wise to dedicate itself only to the emergency at hand: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and the lives of people who need abortions. It is there that they can do the most good for those affected, and it is where they can reshape, as Act Up did, both the public debate and the facts on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Much of this is already happening. In the years that the right has been ascendant and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters have been on the defensive, large, national non-profit advocacy groups have mostly been on the back foot. But in the places they have vacated, a vast network of small but mighty local organizations, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, have stepped in to help those seeking abortions with material needs. These groups represent an essential intervention in material service provision. They can also form the foundation for what feminism needs now: an organized political movement.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220905ei95001mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
