\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Dorothy Roberts on reproductive justice: \u8216\'20\'18 \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only focus\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Marian Jones \par \uc2 1,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2022\par 22:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The scholar discusses why the movement needs to bring reproductive justice out of the margins and into the center\par \par \uc2 For many women of color, the right to control one\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive destiny has always been about much more than the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the recent loss of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, some reproductive rights advocates are calling for a renewed focus on reproductive justice, a concept developed in the early 1990s by women of color. Reproductive justice stresses not just the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also economic, racial and environmental justice, along with other facets of social equality, as critical to true reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 Dorothy Roberts is an internationally renowned scholar of race, gender, and the law at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , who has dedicated her career to exposing attacks on Black women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights dating back to slavery and persisting to the present. She argues that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade validates what reproductive justice advocates have been arguing for years: that the mainstream reproductive rights agenda has been blind to how the state has overpoliced Black women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive lives.\par \par \uc2 Below, she discusses why the reproductive rights movement needs to bring reproductive justice out of the margins and into the center.\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19ve written about the reproductive justice movement, and your book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/155575/killing-the-black-body-by-dorothy-roberts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Killing the Black Body}}}\uc2  is an important contribution to this cause. For those who are unfamiliar, could you explain your work and the movement?\par \par \uc2 In the late 1980s and early 1990s, I began looking into the prosecutions of Black women who were pregnant and taking drugs.\par \par \uc2 These prosecutions were racist and sexist in my opinion, and they were part of a long history of policing and penalizing Black women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive choices, bodies and lives.\par \par \uc2 This work on fighting the criminalization of Black moms and pregnant women during the so-called crack epidemic in the US prompted me to explore the numerous policies that were founded on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.damemagazine.com/2014/07/17/why-do-we-vilify-low-income-black-women-simply-being-mothers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vilifying stereotypes about Black mothers}}}\uc2  and blaming Black women\u8217\'20\'19s childbearing for a range of social ills, and then adopting punitive and villainizing actions.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s what inspired me to write Killing the Black Body .\par \par \uc2 In 1994, while I was writing Killing the Black Body, a group of Black women met at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2019/10/14/fighting-for-abortion-access-in-the-south"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pro-choice conference}}}\uc2  and coined the phrase \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dissentmagazine.org/article/reproductive-justice-not-just-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reproductive justice}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D to describe a holistic approach that puts Black women at the center and incorporates our experiences of having our childbearing decisions punished and devalued. While the mainstream reproductive rights movement focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we expanded the definition of reproductive freedom to include not only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but also [freedom from] state interference. Reproductive justice includes the right to abort a pregnancy and also to raise a child in a safe and supportive community.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.teenvogue.com/story/how-abortion-rights-movement-failed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mainstream pro-choice reproductive rights movement}}}\uc2 , which is led primarily by white women, is associated with organizations such as Planned Parenthood, Naral\par \par \uc2 Pro-Choice America,\par \par \uc2 and the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2 . This movement has largely focused on the legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and reproductive justice has expanded on that. Because of this strategy, what kinds of things has the movement failed to notice or pay attention to?\par \par \uc2 It overlooked the experiences of Black, brown and Indigenous women who have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/document/d/17UIVmNrsVRDdgs47kVZeHCMl3SfjzDf4/edit#bookmark=id.gjdgxs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sterilized}}}\uc2 , abused, or punished for bearing children. It overlooked welfare laws based on misleading impressions of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/history/2013/12/linda_taylor_welfare_queen_ronald_reagan_made_her_a_notorious_american_villain.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 so-called welfare queens}}}\uc2 , Black women who allegedly had babies to collect welfare checks but wasted the money. These stereotypes have led to welfare policies that discourage welfare recipients from having more children by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://imprintnews.org/family/states-repeal-racist-family-cap-welfare/48742"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reducing their benefits}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Black women\u8217\'20\'19s resistance and fight for reproductive rights and health policies pushed me and other Black feminists in the US to challenge the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lux-magazine.com/article/editors-note-3/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 white-dominated reproductive rights}}}\uc2  movement\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rewirenewsgroup.com/article/2017/11/29/beyond-pro-choice-versus-pro-life-qa-radical-reproductive-justice-contributor-andrea-smith/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cchoice\u8221\'20\'1D framework}}}\uc2 . This framework privileges the most socially advantaged people in society who have the wherewithal to make choices. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take into account social structures, power arrangements of race, class, gender, heterosexism, immigration status, religion \u8211\'20\'13 all of which shape one\u8217\'20\'19s ability to have reproductive autonomy.\par \par \uc2 Together with other activists of color, we reframed how we should approach reproductive freedom, away from choice and toward social justice.\par \par \uc2 This approach looks at the circumstances in which people give birth. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/19/black-mothers-birth-maternal-mortality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 High Black maternal mortality}}}\uc2  is a matter of reproductive justice. States that have passed or will soon pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have the worst healthcare systems, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://missouriindependent.com/2022/07/06/states-with-strong-antiabortion-laws-have-high-maternal-and-infant-mortality-rates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the highest maternal mortality}}}\uc2 , especially Black maternal mortality, and the highest infant mortality. As a result of [the supreme court decision] Dobbs, we\u8217\'20\'19ll see increases in maternal mortality \u8211\'20\'13 deaths of pregnant people who intended to carry to term \u8211\'20\'13 because their health will be compromised.\par \par \uc2 It includes ending police violence, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/17/magazine/prison-abolition-ruth-wilson-gilmore.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abolishing prisons}}}\uc2 , and all the inhumane carceral approaches to meeting human needs that have a profound impact on one\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive life. Prisons are a major impediment in the United States to reproductive freedom. People who have had their children taken away by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bostonreview.net/articles/the-benevolent-terror-of-the-child-welfare-system/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a discriminatory child welfare system}}}\uc2  that targets Black neighborhoods for family separation do not have reproductive freedom. To me, reproductive justice is inextricably linked to the fight against the prison industrial complex and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dissentmagazine.org/article/abolish-family-policing-too"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the family policing system}}}\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is important, but not the only focus. But having said that, we also have to understand that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.versobooks.com/blogs/5368-an-attack-on-abortion-is-never-just-about-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bans on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  are connected}}}\uc2  to all these other aspects of reproductive freedom. Reproductive justice activists have been pointing out these connections for decades. I believe the mainstream reproductive rights movement saw them late in the game, but they are painfully clear now.\par \par \uc2 What should the movement do to survive? What concrete strategies would you recommend?\par \par \uc2 Dozens of organizations around the country are focusing more on state and local legislation and courts to support reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 Some of the strategies we could employ include passing legislation that guarantees reproductive freedom and filing cases in state courts. Supporting or organizing efforts and organizations that try to help people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds and organizations that provide information to people seeking care, is just as important. Continuing to protest the aftermath of Dobbs and state legislators\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 All of these are efforts that we can make, in addition to learning about and participating in reproductive justice organizing in general.\par \par \uc2 Who should own the movement now?\par \par \uc2 On one hand, women of color organizing and the reproductive justice framework are more effective than the reproductive choice approach mainstream organizations use. On the other hand, I think we need collective efforts across multiple organizations and struggles. It\u8217\'20\'19s clear that the movement for reproductive justice must be aligned with movements for housing, abolishing the prison industrial complex, environmental justice, and economic justice, because all of those movements are essential to supporting freedom, including reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 Movements seeking to limit or abolish the power of the criminal legal system and the prison industrial complex are relevant to opposing Dobbs\u8217\'20\'19 assault on reproductive freedom. People are already being arrested and imprisoned for stillbirths and miscarriages; that standard will be applied to abortions as well. Recognizing the interconnected nature of these challenges is essential.\par \par \uc2 We should welcome reproductive justice activists\u8217\'20\'19 leadership as we build a more collective movement.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220831ei8v0034u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
