\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Americans are starting to get it: we can\u8217\'20\'19t let Trump \u8211\'20\'13 or Trumpism \u8211\'20\'13 back in office\b0\par\par\uc2 Austin Sarat and Dennis Aftergut \par \uc2 1,113 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2022\par 10:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans have put all their chips on extremism. But voters are sending more and more signals that they\u8217\'20\'19re fed up with it\par \par \uc2 Polls and election results over the last week reminded Americans that politics seldom moves in a straight line. As in physics, action produces reaction. Overreach invites backlash.\par \par \uc2 For a long while former President Trump and his cronies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2020/9/29/the-teflon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seemed to be immune}}}\uc2  from this rule of political life and from the consequences of even the most outrageous conduct. As Trump himself once famously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2016/01/23/politics/donald-trump-shoot-somebody-support"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CI could stand in the middle of Fifth Avenue and shoot somebody and I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t lose voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And so it seemed. He escaped conviction in not one but two impeachment trials and cowed Republican leaders to fall in line after the January 6 insurrection. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/08/20/top-ten-republican-2024-candidates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remains}}}\uc2  the leading contender for the Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 presidential nomination.\par \par \uc2 Today Republicans are still falling over themselves to prove their loyalty to him by outdoing each other in extremism.\par \par \uc2 On 19 August, a Republican candidate for Florida\u8217\'20\'19s state assembly even took to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BillyCorben/status/1560656837266919424?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1560656837266919424%7Ctwgr%5E0131e170bfbb1228a7825939c529301648d56e61%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fnypost.com%2F2022%2F08%2F19%2Ftwitter-bans-floridas-luis-miguel-over-call-to-shoot-fbi-irs-atf-agents%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 call for violence}}}\uc2  against federal law enforcement officials. \u8220\'20\'1CUnder my plan,\u8221\'20\'1D Luis Miguel tweeted, \u8220\'20\'1Call Floridians will have permission to shoot \uc2 FBI\uc2 , \uc2 IRS\uc2 , ATF and all other [federal agents] ON SIGHT! Let freedom ring!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Washington, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  cast aside almost 50 years of settled precedent to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/19-1392_6j37.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 . Republican-dominated state legislatures {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/08/23/1118846811/two-months-after-the-dobbs-ruling-new-abortion-bans-are-taking-hold"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rushed to enact}}}\uc2  draconian restrictions on women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 This kind of extremism may be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2022/08/how-abortion-transformed-democrats-midterm-chances.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 off-putting to swing voters}}}\uc2 . There are signs that most Americans aren\u8217\'20\'19t ready to trade their rights and freedoms for a strongman and his election-denying, rights-infringing, violence-threatening allies. As the Cook Report\u8217\'20\'19s Amy Walters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/analysis/national/national-politics/flip-script"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  on 26 August: \u8220\'20\'1CThe more Trump is in the news, the more dangerous the political climate for the GOP.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But let\u8217\'20\'19s start with the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs sent shock waves across the political spectrum and has jolted Democratic turnout. On 25 August, Axios {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/08/25/democrats-midterms-abortion-turnaround"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  that immediately after Dobbs, \u8220\'20\'1CDemocratic primary turnout for governors\u8217\'20\'19 races increased \u8230\'20\'26 in five of the eight states holding contested primaries.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Similarly, a report from TargetSmart {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://insights.targetsmart.com/not-just-kansas-women-motivated-to-vote-in-states-with-repro-rights-at-risk.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggests}}}\uc2  that in states like Michigan and Wisconsin \u8220\'20\'1Cwhere reproductive rights are at stake\u8221\'20\'1D, women \u8220\'20\'1Care out-registering men by significant margins.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This pattern portends a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.salon.com/2022/08/22/pink-wave-women-rise-up-for-reproductive-rights--and-big-lie-supporters-scramble-to-stop-them/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pink wave}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D in November, as women mobilize to defeat pro-life candidates. We saw evidence of this in the 23 August special congressional election in New York, where Democrat Pat Ryan defeated Republican \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/newsletters/playbook/2022/08/24/dems-flip-2022-on-its-head-00053472"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 52% to 48%}}}\uc2  in a bellwether swing district.\par \par \uc2 Ryan\u8217\'20\'19s campaign message was largely {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/24/democrat-who-campaigned-on-abortion-rights-wins-in-new-york-special-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 focused}}}\uc2  on protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. His victory follows {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/news/our-columnists/the-kansas-abortion-referendum-has-a-message-for-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the striking 2 August referendum vote in Kansas}}}\uc2 , where voters overwhelmingly rejected an attempt to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Are Republicans being taught a lesson they should have learned from history?\par \par \uc2 When the supreme court gets too far out in front of \u8211\'20\'13 or too far behind \u8211\'20\'13 the American public {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/06/27/1107733632/poll-majorities-oppose-supreme-courts-abortion-ruling-and-worry-about-other-righ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by ignoring}}}\uc2  American sentiment, political backlash {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hls.harvard.edu/today/backlash-from-roe-v-wade-continues-to-shape-public-discourse-says-klarman/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 results}}}\uc2 . That {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2022/02/supreme-court-conservative-rulings/622050/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 happened}}}\uc2  in the 1850s in the run-up to the civil war and in the 1930s when the conservative court that Franklin Roosevelt inherited struck down a new minimum wage law.\par \par \uc2 It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/newsplus/backlash-from-roe-v-wade-continues-to-shape-public-discourse/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 happened again}}}\uc2  after Roe v Wade, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes reacted and organized for a 50-year battle that resulted in a reactionary court majority.\par \par \uc2 Republicans may now be reaping what those reactionaries on the court sowed.\par \par \uc2 And it isn\u8217\'20\'19t only that many Americans have been alarmed and aroused by what the court did last June. They are also awakening to the threats posed by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbig lie\u8221\'20\'1D and the election denial it has inspired.\par \par \uc2 Democratic messaging that has called out the \u8220\'20\'1Cbig lie\u8221\'20\'1D, along with the meticulously presented hearings of the January 6 congressional hearings, seem to be taking root.\par \par \uc2 Americans are coming to see that, as President Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/3472826-biden-gets-deadly-earnest-about-threats-to-democracy-amid-jokes-at-correspondents-dinner/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has warned}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CA poison is running through our democracy \u8230\'20\'26 with disinformation massively on the rise. But the truth is buried by lies, and the lies live on as truth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the start of this summer\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 hearings, Republican congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/republican-cheney-warns-us-democracy-remains-under-threat-2022-06-05/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 echoed}}}\uc2  that sentiment: \u8220\'20\'1CPeople must pay attention. People must watch, and they must understand how easily our democratic system can unravel if we don\u8217\'20\'19t defend it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An NBC News poll last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s3.documentcloud.org/documents/22156173/220455-nbc-news-august-poll-82122-release.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggests}}}\uc2  that the American people are indeed now paying attention. It found that more respondents ranked \u8220\'20\'1Cthreats to democracy\u8221\'20\'1D as the most important issue facing the country, more important than inflation or jobs.\par \par \uc2 Other polls suggest that candidates who are running as election deniers or opponents of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/05/us/politics/republicans-abortion-kansas.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will pay a price in November}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take Pennsylvania, for example. A Franklin & Marshall poll released on 25 August {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.getrevue.co/profile/fandmpoll/issues/franklin-marshall-poll-release-august-2022-1324503?via=twitter-card&client=MobileWeb&element=issue-card"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that the Democratic candidate for the Senate, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , is leading Trump-endorsed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/18/us/politics/pa-nc-primary-takeaways.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election denier}}}\uc2  Mehmet Oz, 43% to 30%. Fetterman is also a vocal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporter, while Oz {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2022/jul/18/planned-parenthood-votes/mehmet-oz-has-said-he-supports-more-restrictions-a/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported}}}\uc2  overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 The same poll also shows that the Democratic candidate for governor in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , leads the Trump favorite and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foe, Republican \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , by 44% to 33%.\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/08/15/election-deniers-march-toward-power-key-2024-battlegrounds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CIn 2020, Mastriano tried to block Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s certification of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s victory by introducing a resolution asserting incorrectly that the Republican-dominated legislature had the right to choose which electors\u8217\'20\'19 votes should be counted.\u8221\'20\'1D As the Post also notes, \u8220\'20\'1CHe attended the Jan. 6 riot \u8230\'20\'26 where he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.witf.org/2021/05/28/pa-sen-doug-mastriano-unlikely-to-face-consequences-over-newly-released-jan-6-videos/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was captured on video}}}\uc2  crossing the police line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This is not to say that in Pennsylvania or elsewhere the Trump fever has completely broken. And polls are not the same thing as an election. But they are signs of hope.\par \par \uc2 Democracy won\u8217\'20\'19t save itself. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will not restore themselves. The American majority\u8217\'20\'19s power to defeat Trumpism lies at the ballot box. If Trumpist candidates lose in general elections, over time Republicans may get the message that they\u8217\'20\'19ve placed a losing bet on extremism.\par \par \uc2 There is much to be done by Americans committed to preserving our republic and to saying \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D to Trump. As former president Obama {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/Farewell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 put it}}}\uc2  in his 2017 farewell address: \u8220\'20\'1CIt falls to each of us to be \u8230\'20\'26 jealous guardians of democracy.\u8221\'20\'1D Across America, a majority of voters are ready to do just that.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Austin Sarat is a professor of jurisprudence and political science at \uc2 Amherst College\uc2  and the author of Gruesome Spectacles: Botched Executions and America\u8217\'20\'19s Death Penalty\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Dennis Aftergut is a former federal prosecutor, currently of counsel to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://ldad.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lawyers Defending American Democracy}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220830ei8u001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
