\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Slew of trigger laws kick in as three more US states ban abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 1,116 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2022\par 11:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tennessee, Texas and Idaho join eight other states as millions of women will lose access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in certain cases doctors will be punished for performing procedure\par \par \uc2 A slew of trigger bans across three US states kicked in on Thursday as Tennessee, Texas and Idaho join eight other states that have formally outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 Depending on the state, trigger laws are designed to take effect either immediately following the overturn of Roe or 30 days after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s transmission of its judgment, which took place on 26 July.\par \par \uc2 Currently, nearly one in three women between the ages of 15 to 44 live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been banned or mostly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2022/08/22/more-trigger-bans-loom-1-3-women-lose-most-abortion-access-post-roe/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banned.}}}\uc2  According to data obtained by the US census, that is nearly 21 million women affected.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMore people will lose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the nation as bans take effect in Texas, Tennessee and Idaho. Vast swaths of the nation, especially in the south and midwest, will become \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  deserts that, for many, will be impossible to escape,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , CEO of the Center of Reproductive Rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/abortion-trigger-bans-take-effect-in-three-states-tomorrow/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvidence is already mounting of women being turned away despite needing urgent, and in some cases life-saving, medical care. This unfolding public health crisis will only continue to get worse. We will see more and more of these harrowing situations, and once state legislatures reconvene in January, we will see even more states implement \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and novel laws criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, pregnant people, and those who help them,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s trigger bans strip away the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for millions of women in Tennessee, Texas and Idaho and in certain cases punish doctors and healthcare providers for performing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, the state\u8217\'20\'19s previous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that bans the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy has been replaced with a stricter law. Aside from the exception of preventing the mother\u8217\'20\'19s death or permanent bodily injury, the law bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely. It does not make any exceptions for victims of incest or rape.\par \par \uc2 The law, called the Human Life Protection Act, makes it a felony for those who are caught performing or attempting to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Consequences include fines, prison time and the loss of voting rights.\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitol.tn.gov/Bills/111/Bill/SB1257.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 law}}}\uc2 , abortions are prohibited from being performed based on mental health claims, including claims that the woman may \u8220\'20\'1Cengage in conduct that would result in her death or substantial and irreversible impairment of a major bodily function\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Texas, which already passed one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws last yearbanning the procedure beyond six weeks of pregnancy and offering no exceptions for incest or rape, will see a new trigger law take effect that makes the provision of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a first-degree felony. Consequences include life sentences and a civil penalty of $100,000 for each violation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe criminal penalties will further chill the provision of care to women who need it,\u8221\'20\'1D Elisabeth Smith, director of state policy and advocacy for the Center of Productive Rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2022/08/22/more-trigger-bans-loom-1-3-women-lose-most-abortion-access-post-roe/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s trigger ban comes a day after a federal judge in the state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/24/texas-judge-blocks-emergency-abortion-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2  an order from the Biden administration issued in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overruling of Roe that required hospitals to provide emergency abortions.\par \par \uc2 According to Judge James Hendrix, a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 -appointee, the US Department of Health and Human Services overreached in its guidance interpreting the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labour Act. The 1986 law, also known as Emtala, requires people to receive emergency medical care regardless of their ability to pay for the services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat guidance goes well beyond EMTALA\u8217\'20\'19s text, which protects both mothers and unborn children, is silent as to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and preempts state law only when the two directly conflict,\u8221\'20\'1D Hendrix {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.txnd.365015/gov.uscourts.txnd.365015.73.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  in a 67-page ruling.\par \par \uc2 The White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , condemned the decision, calling it a \u8220\'20\'1Ca blow to Texans\u8221\'20\'1D, and adding, \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s wrong, it\u8217\'20\'19s backwards, and women may die as a result. The fight is not over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In North Dakota, a judge blocked a trigger law after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic argued that the law violates the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitutions.\par \par \uc2 Burleigh county District Judge Bruce Romanick granted the request for a preliminary injunction as part of a lawsuit brought by the Red River Women\u8217\'20\'19s Clinic in Fargo.\par \par \uc2 The ban was set to take effect on Friday. The clinic already moved its services a short distance to neighboring Moorhead, Minnesota, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, even as it seeks to block the North Dakota law.\par \par \uc2 Romanick said he was not ruling on the probability of the clinic winning the lawsuit, rather that more time was needed to make a proper judgment. He said that even though the clinic moved its operations to Minnesota, the statute would also affect doctors and hospitals, making the decision to delay \u8220\'20\'1Cstill pertinent and appropriate\u8221\'20\'1D, the judge said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe right of women to make the decisions affecting their personal autonomy should be guaranteed by the North Dakota constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tom Dickson, the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s attorney.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit argues that the state constitution\u8217\'20\'19s guarantees of rights to life, liberty, safety and happiness effectively guarantee a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the second time that Romanick has put the trigger ban on hold.\par \par \uc2 Abortions in Idaho were previously limited to a six-week period into pregnancy. However, Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s trigger law completely prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the exceptions of reported cases of rape and incest and to prevent the death of the mother \u8211\'20\'13 but not necessarily to safeguard her health.\par \par \uc2 The ban makes performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any \u8220\'20\'1Cclinically diagnosable pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D a felony that is punishable by up to five years of jail time.\par \par \uc2 Despite the sweeping ban, an Idaho judge barred the state at the 11th hour from enforcing its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in medical emergencies, making the ruling the exact opposite of Hendrix\u8217\'20\'19s decision in Texas. The ruling from federal judge Lynn Winmill on Wednesday evening says that the state cannot prosecute anyone who performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an emergency medical situation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt its core, the supremacy clause says state law must yield to federal law when it\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to comply with both. And that\u8217\'20\'19s all this case is about,\u8221\'20\'1D Winmill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/25/idaho-judge-bars-state-enforcing-abortion-ban-medical-emergencies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote.}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not about the bygone constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 With such conflicting rulings, both cases could be appealed and the supreme court may be asked to intervene.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed to this report.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220825ei8p002s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{