\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  is still legal in Arizona. But confusion and fear abound\b0\par\par\uc2 Nina Lakhani \par \uc2 1,371 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par 12:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision ending the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has led to legal chaos and a de facto ban for many\par \par \uc2 A day after the supreme court upended reproductive freedom by overturning Roe v Wade, nine out of Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s 10 clinics stopped providing abortions.\par \par \uc2 Arizona was not among the states with trigger laws that automatically banned most abortions after the ruling in June reversed 50 years of legal precedent, yet a slew of confusing and contradictory laws meant \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care became virtually impossible to access anyway.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood, which has four clinics in the state\u8217\'20\'19s three largest cities, has not resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care due to the legal chaos \u8211\'20\'13 signaling a de facto ban that has forced hundreds of women and girls to travel out of state, seek treatment online or continue unwanted pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur elected officials are playing politics with patients. This comes straight out of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  playbook and unfortunately it\u8217\'20\'19s been successful in sowing chaos, confusion and fear among providers and patients in the state,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brittany Fonteno, president of Planned Parenthood Arizona, which used to provide about 5,000 abortions annually.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most circumstances remains legal until 20 weeks of gestation. Yet within hours of the supreme court decision overturning Roe, some Republican officials falsely claimed that a total ban was back in place. The confusion has forced many clinics to suspend or reduce the services they can provide patients.\par \par \uc2 Dr Deshawn Taylor runs the independent Desert Star family planning clinic in Phoenix and has provided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care for more than 21 years. For her, it was a tough decision to suspend services but being a Black doctor made her feel particularly vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that Black people are the first to be criminalised in this country \u8230\'20\'26 I\u8217\'20\'19m a Black woman, all my staff and many of our patients are people of color. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal but that would not stop someone from causing a legal disaster that I would not be able to recover from,\u8221\'20\'1D Taylor told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Taylor restarted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in mid-July, but is offering a muchreduced service \u8211\'20\'13 in large part due to staff concerns fueled by misinformation and uncertainty surrounding the legal situation. (One person left, another cannot be convinced that they won\u8217\'20\'19t face criminal charges, and it has been impossible to recruit new staff.)\par \par \uc2 The clinic now offers only medical abortions, whereas previously Taylor provided 90 to 100 surgical abortions a month to patients who needed a later termination and those who simply preferred the surgical procedure because it\u8217\'20\'19s faster, more reliable and less painful.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s extremely frustrating that I can\u8217\'20\'19t help my patients to the full extent because of the confusion and misinformation \u8230\'20\'26 I\u8217\'20\'19m absolutely sure that more women are being forced to continue pregnancies,\u8221\'20\'1D said Taylor.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The post-Roe landscape in the US can be loosely divided into three categories: states that have implemented total (or near-total) bans, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -friendly states struggling to keep up with the increased demand, and states \u8211\'20\'13 like Arizona \u8211\'20\'13 where the confusing legal situation has reduced access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, three laws are being wrangled over:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A so-called personhood law\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A ban after 15 weeks due to take effect in late September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A 19th-century law banning all abortions\par \par \uc2 In 2021, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2021/04/27/arizona-anti-abortion-bill-becomes-law/4855859001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a two-part anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bill }}}\uc2  was signed into law by Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  which gave fetuses rights from the moment of conception \u8211\'20\'13 a so-called personhood law \u8211\'20\'13 and made it a crime for doctors to provide abortions for foetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Roe made the personhood law unconstitutional by default, while a court blocked the foetal abnormality exclusion. Doctors now face prison time if they terminate pregnancies solely on the basis of non-fatal conditions like cystic fibrosis or Down\u8217\'20\'19s syndrome.\par \par \uc2 When Roe fell, advocates quickly filed a lawsuit to block the personhood law, and won after a federal judge last month ruled that the law is too vague and risks arbitrary enforcement. In court filings, the state attorney general, Mark {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2022/07/11/judge-issues-injunction-arizona-anti-abortion-personhood-law/10034656002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brnovich, admitted that it was \u8220\'20\'1Canyone\u8217\'20\'19s guess\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  how the law applies. The injunction has not yet been appealed.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Governor Ducey signed another law that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/2022/03/30/gov-ducey-signs-bill-outlawing-abortions-after-15-weeks-arizona/7223681001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  bans almost all abortions after 15 weeks}}}\uc2  except in extreme medical cases \u8211\'20\'13 similar to the Mississippi law that led to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs ruling.\par \par \uc2 The 15-week legislation will come into force around 24 September \u8211\'20\'13 unless a court rules that a law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azleg.gov/ars/13/03603.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dating back to 1864 banning all abortions}}}\uc2  unless deemed medically necessary to save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life is in fact still valid. The pre-statehood legislation has been condemned as \u8220\'20\'1Cdraconian\u8221\'20\'1D by advocates and carries a mandatory two- to five-year prison sentence for anyone who helps or provides a termination.\par \par \uc2 Republicans disagree over which law takes precedence, with Ducey arguing for the recent 15-week law, while Brnovich has claimed that the total ban is in effect. Brnovich has also asked the court to lift the post-Roe injunction on the ban \u8220\'20\'1Cin order to provide further clarity and uniformity\u8221\'20\'1D, according to Brittni Thomason, spokesperson for the AG\u8217\'20\'19s office.\par \par \uc2 Thomason points out that the pre-statehood law was re-codified by the legislature in 1977, and recent legislation explicitly states that the law remains valid and not repealed. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile there will always be people who try to shoot the messenger, Brnovich\u8217\'20\'19s fidelity to the rule of law is beyond reproach.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s confusing, but there\u8217\'20\'19s more: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/state-facts-about-abortion-arizona"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legislation chipping away at \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access}}}\uc2  has been passed by the Republican-controlled legislature and governor every year bar one since 2009. (Before that Democrats controlled the governorship for 12 years and vetoed several bills.)\par \par \uc2 Advocates argue that this slew of restrictive laws is in fact evidence that the legislature has for years implicitly recognised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a legal regulated medical procedure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArizona is the textbook example of chaos and uncertainty, with providers and patients trying to figure out the law. It\u8217\'20\'19s a mess. The attorney general has refused to provide clarity, and you have to conclude that\u8217\'20\'19s because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents want providers to be conservative in the way they offer care to avoid legal risks,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , state policy expert at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A hearing next Friday will help decide the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the Grand Canyon state. It remains to be seen whether the court will rule to validate a total ban first enacted before Arizona became a state, but what\u8217\'20\'19s clear is that further restrictions to access are inevitable. For now, the best-case scenario is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is reduced from 20 to 15 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe 1864 law the attorney general is trying to enforce is cruel, draconian and completely out of step with what the vast majority of Arizonans want. We hope the judge will give us some clarity,\u8221\'20\'1D said Fonteno from Planned Parenthood, who will argue against the total ban being resurrected.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kjzz.org/content/1783135/poll-most-arizona-voters-want-abortion-remain-legal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Almost 90% of Arizonans }}}\uc2  want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain legal, at least in some circumstances.\par \par \uc2 In any case, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will almost certainly end up in the state supreme court, which the outgoing governor has packed with a conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 One thing that everyone agrees is that there is a lot hanging on the November midterms and 2023 legislative session in Arizona \u8211\'20\'13 and across the country. The Trump-backed Republican candidate for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/07/10/arizona-attorney-general-candidates-share-their-stances-abortion/7706590001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  attorney general, Abe Hamadeh}}}\uc2 , has said the total ban is the state law, while the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/29/kris-mayes-arizona-attorney-general-voting-2022-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrat candidate, Kris Mayes, }}}\uc2  has said she considers all three laws under dispute to be unconstitutional and will not enforce them.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  expects more states to ban and restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access next year, with some Republican-controlled legislatures likely to implement overlapping restrictions in an effort to make access impossible inside and outside the state. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very scary time, and people\u8217\'20\'19s lives and futures do hang in the balance,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nash.\par \par \uc2 Dr Taylor said: \u8220\'20\'1CMy biggest fear is that after all the righteous indignation, people don\u8217\'20\'19t show up to vote in November and we become the next Texas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220824ei8o002z3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
