\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 hopes rise for midterms amid backlash over  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 1,098 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par 17:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 30\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrat Pat Ryan made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  central to his campaign, and defeated Republican Mark Molinaro in contested House election\par \par \uc2 After months of bracing for staggering losses, Democrats are suddenly filled with a sense of optimism that the 2022 midterm elections could defy historical precedent and see them perform strongly, especially by highlighting the Republican threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday night, Democrat Pat Ryan defeated Republican Mark Molinaro in a fiercely contested special House election in New York\u8217\'20\'19s Hudson Valley, in a contest that was seen as a barometer of impact of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to end the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ryan, a combat veteran, made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the centerpiece of his campaign, making the case that he fought to protect American freedoms abroad only to see them stripped away at home.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, Molinaro adopted the usual Republican messaging, attempting to turn the election into a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the unpopular Democratic president. On the campaign trail, he focused on voters\u8217\'20\'19 anxieties over inflation and crime and rarely mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Outcome reveals how a brewing backlash over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access could dramatically reshape the American electoral landscape\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe got in this race because the foundations of our democracy were and remain under direct threat \u8211\'20\'13 and that is deadly serious,\u8221\'20\'1D Ryan said in a victory speech \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the supreme court ripped away reproductive freedoms, access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, we said \u8216\'20\'18this is not what America stands for.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The outcome reveals how a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/22/democrats-abortion-rights-midterms-november"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brewing backlash}}}\uc2  over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access could be dramatically reshaping the American electoral landscape this year.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have now outperformed Biden in four House special elections since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s June decision in Dobbs v Jackson, and even voters in traditionally conservative Kansas overwhelmingly voted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a referendum there. Across the country, Democratic candidates have leaned into the issue and are now lashing their Republican opponents over their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, but the issue was rarely a motivating factor in voters\u8217\'20\'19 decision. New polling suggests that is changing in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision. A new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2022/08/23/abortion-rises-in-importance-as-a-voting-issue-driven-by-democrats/?utm_source=AdaptiveMailer&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=8-23-22%20Midterms%20GEN%20DISTRO&org=982&lvl=100&ite=10352&lea=2176602&ctr=0&par=1&trk=a0D3j000011f2X8EAI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found that 56% of voters say the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be \u8220\'20\'1Cvery important\u8221\'20\'1D to their decision this November, up from 43% who said the same in March.\par \par \uc2 It also comes as Democrats celebrate what the White House chief of staff, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , called a \u8220\'20\'1Cseason of substance\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 big legislative wins on climate and healthcare that achieve several of his party\u8217\'20\'19s long-sought policy goals. Average gas prices are falling and there are signs that inflation \u8211\'20\'13 while still painfully high \u8211\'20\'13 may finally be moderating.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans can say goodbye to their \u8216\'20\'18Red Wave\u8217\'20\'19 because voters are clearly coming out in force to elect a pro-choice majority to Congress this November,\u8221\'20\'1D the New York congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Maloney had another reason to celebrate on Tuesday night: the congressman soundly defeated his progressive challenger in one of several closely watched contests on Tuesday, the last major primary night of the election season.\par \par \uc2 The races in New York, Florida and Oklahoma helped set the stage for several key races that will determine control of Congress, governor\u8217\'20\'19s offices and state legislatures around the country.\par \par \uc2 Though aggressive Republican gerrymandering in Florida expanded conservatives\u8217\'20\'19 march in the state, Democrats hope their prospects statewide are brighter. On Tuesday, Democratic congressman \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  prevailed over a more liberal challenger to take on Republican governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Crist, the one-time Republican governor of Florida, has positioned himself as a unifier in the mold of Biden, a sharp contrast to DeSantis, a cultural warrior seen as a potential 2024 presidential contender. To take on Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , Democrats chose Congresswoman Val Demings, a former police chief in Orlando and a rising political star in her party.\par \par \uc2 But taking on two of the biggest names in Florida \u8211\'20\'13 and Republican politics \u8211\'20\'13 will be no easy task for the Democrats in the red-leaning battleground.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe stakes could not be any higher for this election,\u8221\'20\'1D Crist said in a victory speech on Tuesday night. \u8220\'20\'1COur fundamental freedoms are literally on the ballot, a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose, democracy is on the ballot.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Across Florida, several far-right candidates prevailed in a sign of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s enduring influence over his party. Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , one of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s most vocal defenders in the House, won his primary despite facing a federal investigation into sex trafficking allegations.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, DeSantis, viewed as a potential rival to Trump in Florida, used his sway to successfully back roughly a dozen far-right local school board candidates, a move sure to roil the already fierce national debate over education policy, LGBTQ rights and teaching racism in classrooms.\par \par \uc2 In one of the fiercest intra-party contests, Congressman \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  routed Congresswoman \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  in a bitter primary that pitted two long-serving and powerful Democratic chairs against one another for a newly drawn Manhattan district.\par \par \uc2 The redistricting scramble in the Empire State pushed Democratic congressman \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 , one of the first two openly gay, Black members of Congress, to run in a new district. He lost in a crowded primary on Tuesday night to Dan Goldman, a former federal prosecutor who served as the lead counsel for House Democrats during Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first impeachment.\par \par \uc2 There were wins for establishment Republicans as well. In New York, \uc2 Nick Langworthy\uc2 , chair of the state Republican party, beat Carl Paladino, a businessman and former Republican nominee for governor with a history of racist, incendiary remarks. And in Florida, Congressman Daniel Webster held off a challenge from Laura Loomer, a far-right activist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2020/08/19/twitter-keeps-ban-laura-loomer-398695"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banned from Twitter}}}\uc2  for her anti-Muslim posts.\par \par \uc2 In conservative Oklahoma, a Trump-backed congressman, \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2 , won a runoff election to become the Republican nominee in the race to succeed the retiring Republican senator \uc2 James Inhofe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Progressives prevailed in a contentious Florida primary with the nomination of Maxwell Alejandro Frost in a Democratic stronghold. Frost, 25, a liberal activist who drives Uber for extra money, would be the first Gen Z member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the co-chairs of the House Progressive Caucus hailed Frost as a \u8220\'20\'1Cbold, fresh progressive voice\u8221\'20\'1D in Congress: \u8220\'20\'1CMaxwell represents a generation who have grown up in the shadow of the climate crisis, economic unfairness, and gun violence and channeled that into becoming grassroots leaders and champions for progressive change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220824ei8o002ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
