\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas judge blocks Biden order requiring hospitals to provide emergency abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Edwin Rios \par \uc2 431 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par 13:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump-appointed judge says US health department overreached in its guidance, in decision issued just before \u8216\'20\'18trigger law\u8217\'20\'19 takes effect\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Texas has blocked a Biden administration guidance that required hospitals to provide emergency abortions, even in states like Texas, which prohibits the practice following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The legal effort by the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , a stalwart Republican, represents the latest attempt to stop the federal government from influencing the reproductive access landscape in the aftermath of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision, which overturned longstanding constitutional protections on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Such preventions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access could have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/02/cost-banning-abortion-financial-emotional-comic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 devastating }}}\uc2  financial and health consequences on women, especially {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/19/black-mothers-birth-maternal-mortality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/27/roe-v-wade-overturned-maternal-mortality-rate-will-rise"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latino and Indigenous women}}}\uc2  who already disproportionately suffer from deaths during childbirth.\par \par \uc2 The decision effectively left it to states to decide whether to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 As a result, abortions bans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/08/23/1118846811/two-months-after-the-dobbs-ruling-new-abortion-bans-are-taking-hold"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have spread}}}\uc2  throughout Republican-led states like Idaho and Tennessee, even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups leverage the US courts to maintain reproductive health protections under state constitutions. In Kansas, voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved}}}\uc2  a measure to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the deeply conservative state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution, striking a blow to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 In his decision, Judge James Wesley Hendrix, who was appointed by Donald Trump, concluded that the US Department of Health and Human Services overreached in its guidance interpreting the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cms.gov/Regulations-and-Guidance/Legislation/EMTALA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1986 law}}}\uc2  that requires people receive emergency medical care regardless of their ability to pay.\par \par \uc2 Hendrix stopped short of blocking the guidance nationally, keeping the prohibition in Texas. The decision sets the stage for another anticipated ruling in a justice department lawsuit against a recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Idaho that involves the same federal law at the heart of the Texas case, Reuters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/us-judge-blocks-federal-guidance-emergency-abortions-texas-2022-08-24/?utm_medium=Social&utm_source=twitter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 . That ruling is expected on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat guidance goes well beyond EMTALA\u8217\'20\'19s text, which protects both mothers and unborn children, is silent as to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and preempts state law only when the two directly conflict,\u8221\'20\'1D Hendrix wrote.\par \par \uc2 Hendrix\u8217\'20\'19s decision came just one day before a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D barring nearly all abortions went into effect on Wednesday. The law, passed by the Texas legislature in 2021, increases the criminal and civil penalties on those involved with abortions except the pregnant patient, the Texas Tribune {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/07/26/texas-abortion-ban-dobbs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 . Since the Dobbs decision, clinics have stopped offering services, forcing people to travel to other states to seek out abortions.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220824ei8o0025w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
