\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Florida calls teen \u8216\'20\'18too immature\u8217\'20\'19 for an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 . But forced motherhood is fine?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jill Filipovic \par \uc2 1,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2022\par 14:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Florida court\u8217\'20\'19s decision is shameful, nothing less than an ideological assault on a teenager\par \par \uc2 A Florida court has held that a pregnant and parentless 16-year-old is not mature enough to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but is, apparently, mature enough to raise a child after being forced into childbirth by the state.\par \par \uc2 This case illustrates the utter absurdity and deep cruelty of parental consent laws, which are in place {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/parental-involvement-minors-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in 21 states}}}\uc2 . While parental consent laws seem reasonable enough on their face \u8211\'20\'13 who wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t want to know if their child was having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? \u8211\'20\'13 they are in practice alternately duplicative or dangerous. If a parent is supportive of their child\u8217\'20\'19s bodily autonomy and has raised that child into a thoughtful, mature teenager, that teenager will either feel comfortable telling their parents about an unplanned pregnancy, or will have the ability and wherewithal to make their own decision and get themselves to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. If a parent is abusive or unsupportive of their child\u8217\'20\'19s basic rights, why should the state put young people at risk by requiring that their parents be notified?\par \par \uc2 The reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that it essentially maintains the status quo. A teenager who ends her pregnancy does not risk her life in childbirth. She is able to continue her schooling or her work without interruption. She is able to remain on whatever path she was walking before her pregnancy, and she can pursue her goals and dreams on the schedule she had in mind.\par \par \uc2 Making the decision to keep one\u8217\'20\'19s life moving forward on the same path requires far less maturity than making the decision to become a parent \u8211\'20\'13 a decision the state does not interfere with, and for good reason. If the idea of a parent or a panel of judges forcing a teenage girl into an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against her will disgusts and outrages you, so should the idea of a parent or panel of judges forcing a girl to go through the much more dangerous, physically demanding and permanently life-altering process of giving birth and becoming a mother.\par \par \uc2 While an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a medical procedure, ending a pregnancy is overwhelmingly safe, and a woman is about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-abortion/abortion-safer-than-giving-birth-study-idUSTRE80M2BS20120123"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14 times more likely}}}\uc2  to die of complications from childbirth than from complications from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And yet the state \u8211\'20\'13 rightly \u8211\'20\'13 does not require pregnant teenagers to get their parents\u8217\'20\'19 permission to carry to term, nor allow parents to force their children into abortions against their will.\par \par \uc2 In the situation playing out in Florida, the teenager girl in question does not have parents in the picture. Her guardian is supportive of her choice to end her pregnancy. She has managed to get herself into court and before a panel of judges \u8211\'20\'13 that is how badly she does not want to be forced into continued pregnancy, childbirth and parenthood.\par \par \uc2 And she is right that having a child before she\u8217\'20\'19s ready could be devastating. She is reportedly working on obtaining her GED, but it\u8217\'20\'19s infinitely more difficult to study when you\u8217\'20\'19ve just given birth, or when you\u8217\'20\'19re the sole caregiver for an infant. She lives with a guardian, but she will be a legal adult in less than two years\u8217\'20\'19 time. The man or boy who impregnated her seems to have no intention of being involved; she has no parents to help her, and it sounds like very little in the way of a familial safety net. She is going to need money for diapers, wipes, baby accoutrements, food, transport, childcare and eventually a place to live for herself and a child. How is a single teenage mother without a high school education and no parental support, and who has been forced to endure the trauma of unwanted pregnancy and birth, going to adequately support herself, let alone a child, under these circumstances?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/research/ongoing/turnaway-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Researchers have found}}}\uc2  that when women are denied abortions and forced instead to give birth, the results are dire. They are much more likely to remain in poverty than women who were able to end their pregnancies. They are more likely to be tethered to abusive men. They end up in worse health. Their children are worse off by many measures. And they are more likely to die.\par \par \uc2 And these poor outcomes are only magnified when the people in question are teenagers. While {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/up-front/2021/01/12/the-unreported-gender-gap-in-high-school-graduation-rates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly 90%}}}\uc2  of American girls graduate from high school, only about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/teenpregnancy/about/index.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 50%}}}\uc2  of teenage mothers do. While about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2022/04/12/10-facts-about-todays-college-graduates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 four in ten American}}}\uc2  women have a bachelor\u8217\'20\'19s degree, only about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://youth.gov/youth-topics/pregnancy-prevention/adverse-effects-teen-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in 10 women}}}\uc2  who gave birth as teenagers has finished at either a two- or four-year college.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant adolescents are more likely than adult women to experience {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17978452"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a host of health- and life-threatening pregnancy complications}}}\uc2 , including pregnancy-induced hypertension, pre-eclampsia and eclampsia. They are more likely to go into labor early, and more likely to have a lower birthweight baby. Their babies are more likely to die. Their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/teenpregnancy/about/index.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 children}}}\uc2  also do worse in school, are less likely to graduate high school, are less likely to be employed in adulthood, are on average in poorer health, and are more likely to be placed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://youth.gov/youth-topics/pregnancy-prevention/adverse-effects-teen-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 foster care}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 None of this needs to be a foregone conclusion, of course. States could invest much more heavily in healthcare and support for all families, and for low-income families and those led by single parents in particular. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/28/upshot/abortion-bans-states-social-services.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  states}}}\uc2  overwhelmingly do not support pregnant women, children and families nearly as robustly as progressive pro-choice ones. Florida is the 46th worst-ranked state in the nation when it comes to the percentage of uninsured women; it\u8217\'20\'19s the 29th worst in maternal mortality, 30th in infant mortality and 33rd in child poverty. It offers no paid leave for new mothers.\par \par \uc2 Teenage girls and adult women alike, no matter how allegedly \u8220\'20\'1Cimmature\u8221\'20\'1D, are overwhelmingly capable of looking at their lives and deciding, \u8220\'20\'1Ccarrying this pregnancy to term would make things worse, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to do it\u8221\'20\'1D. Being able to make that assessment is itself a sign of maturity \u8211\'20\'13 certainly much more so than deciding one is ready to care for a baby when one is not yet able to fully care for themselves.\par \par \uc2 The Florida court\u8217\'20\'19s decision is shameful, nothing less than an ideological assault on a teenager. She knows exactly what the stakes are here, and yet three adults who are strangers to her have decided to force her \u8211\'20\'13 a child they have deemed immature and incapable of making her own decisions \u8211\'20\'13 to become a mother, in charge not only of herself but of a vulnerable infant.\par \par \uc2 Decisions like this one may very well drive young people to bypass the courts entirely and take matters into their own hands \u8211\'20\'13 a worse and potentially far more dangerous outcome. At the very least, this decision will curtail a teenager\u8217\'20\'19s future, compromise her health, force her to suffer through one of the most painful processes human beings endure, threaten her life and the life of her child, and then charge her with the lifelong care of a new person.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a lot to put on a girl who allegedly isn\u8217\'20\'19t mature enough to have a medical procedure utilized by one in four women worldwide. Not mature enough for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but mature enough for a child? It simply defies reason. And that is, perhaps, because it\u8217\'20\'19s not about reason or maturity at all. It\u8217\'20\'19s about control \u8211\'20\'13 and a court imposing its misogynist anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ideology on a teenager who came to them for help.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic is the author of the The H-Spot: The Feminist Pursuit of Happiness\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220819ei8j002h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
