\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 How private is your period-tracking app? Not very, study reveals\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul \par \uc2 627 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par 11:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Research on more than 20 apps found that the majority collected large amounts of personal data and shared it with third parties\par \par \uc2 After the fall of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the US, pressure has mounted on apps that collect pregnancy-related data to preserve people\u8217\'20\'19s privacy. A new study has found many of them do not hold up to scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 Experts at internet research non-profit Mozilla studied more than 20 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://foundation.mozilla.org/en/blog/why-mozilla-is-scrutinizing-the-privacy-of-pregnancy-apps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pregnancy and period tracking apps}}}\uc2  for privacy and security features and said the results were grim.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of these products collect vast amounts of personal data, and then share it widely,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ashley Boyd, the vice-president of advocacy at Mozilla.\par \par \uc2 Of the 10 pregnancy apps, 10 period trackers and five wearables reviewed in the study, only seven were deemed to have safe user data and privacy practices. Most collected large amounts of personal data and shared it with third parties such as data brokers and advertisers. The study also examined security practices and found eight apps failed to meet minimum security? ?standards and allowed weak passwords. Many apps also offered unclear policies surrounding police warrants for user data or made no stance on such requests.\par \par \uc2 Mozilla has published the report, called Privacy Not Included, for nearly a decade, but its results have taken on new urgency after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade this summer, effectively ending the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. The decision immediately raised concerns about the tech industry\u8217\'20\'19s potential compliance with the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Period tracking apps are used by nearly one in three women in the US, according to a 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/other/poll-finding/kff-health-apps-and-information-survey/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  published by the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , logging large swaths of information about length of menstrual cycle, types of birth control used, and other health issues.\par \par \uc2 Experts fear this data could be used by law enforcement officials to prosecute people illegally seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While there is not yet evidence period tracking data is being used in investigations, other tech companies are already contending with law enforcement requests. Last week, it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/10/facebook-user-data-abortion-nebraska-police"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was revealed a 17-year-old}}}\uc2  teen and her mother in Nebraska are facing criminal charges after \uc2 Facebook\uc2  handed over data including private messages related to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the girl had obtained illegally.\par \par \uc2 The majority of the apps studied had \u8220\'20\'1Cmisleading\u8221\'20\'1D data-sharing policies and lacked clear guidelines on how data requests from law enforcement would be handled, the study showed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of these apps share data with a large number of third parties, and that includes everyone from advertisers and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to research partners and law enforcement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mozilla researcher Jen Caltrider. \u8220\'20\'1CThis raises a lot of questions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mozilla divided the apps into the camps of \u8220\'20\'1Cnot creepy\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cvery creepy\u8221\'20\'1D, and labeled those carrying significant privacy concerns as \u8220\'20\'1Cprivacy not included\u8221\'20\'1D. Only three apps and four wearable devices of the more than 20 surveyed made the cut, including period tracking app Euki and \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Fitbit\uc2  device.\par \par \uc2 Euki was described as \u8220\'20\'1Ca sexual and reproductive health app designed with privacy in mind\u8221\'20\'1D and does not collect any personal data that could be investigated by law enforcement or obtained by other entities. Other apps were not so clear, the study showed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of these privacy policies feature very vague statements about whether the app will share data with law enforcement, and those gray areas are going to be increasingly exploited,\u8221\'20\'1D Caltrider said.\par \par \uc2 Researchers are encouraging users to read up before they choose a tracking app, and to avoid apps that collect large amounts of data regardless of what privacy practices they advertise.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow more than ever, consumers need to be empowered when it comes to privacy,\u8221\'20\'1D the study said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220817ei8h0015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
