\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Victorian Labor government votes against bill to widen access to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Adeshola Ore \par \uc2 759 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par 03:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The bill aimed to prevent publicly funded hospitals from refusing to perform abortions across the stateFollow our Australia news live blog for the latest updatesGet our free news app, morning email briefing or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Labor government has voted against a bill that aimed to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in publicly funded hospitals across the state.\par \par \uc2 Crossbench MP {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/aug/16/crossbench-mp-prepares-to-wedge-victorian-government-on-abortion-access-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fiona Patten brought on debate}}}\uc2  in the upper house for the bill that sought to prevent publicly funded hospitals, including those with religious affiliations, from refusing to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Patten\u8217\'20\'19s bill, introduced after the overturning of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade decision}}}\uc2  in the US that led to abortions being banned in multiple states, also aimed to stop hospitals that receive public funding from refusing to perform assisted dying procedures for terminally ill patients.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian government and the opposition used their numbers in the parliament\u8217\'20\'19s upper house to vote against the bill on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Patten \u8211\'20\'13 the leader of the Reason party \u8211\'20\'13 said the outcome was a loss for women and gender diverse Victorians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRight now, woman and gender diverse people in Australia need to know that the government has their back in regards to reproductive rights and in regards to assisted dying,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd the opposition and the government did not give them that assurance today. It was a a win for religious ideology.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Patten said she was concerned that Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s parliament could look \u8220\'20\'1Cvery different\u8221\'20\'1D after the November election, after the Liberal party\u8217\'20\'19s recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/aug/03/moira-deeming-pictured-alongside-liberal-colleague-at-anti-abortion-zoom-meeting-during-melbourne-lockdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pre-selection of conservative upper house candidates}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe may well see these rights under attack,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian government has a practice not to support any private members\u8217\'20\'19 bills.\par \par \uc2 The government\u8217\'20\'19s parliamentary secretary for health, Nina Taylor, said Labor had a positive record on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, pointing to safe access zones around clinics and a $19.4m commitment in the May budget to strengthen female health services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVictoria is Australia\u8217\'20\'19s most progressive state when it comes to women\u8217\'20\'19s rights and access to reproductive choices,\u8221\'20\'1D Taylor told parliament.\par \par \uc2 Taylor said the government would continue to take steps to improve women\u8217\'20\'19s and gender diverse people\u8217\'20\'19s access to health care.\par \par \uc2 The opposition\u8217\'20\'19s health spokesperson, Georgie Crozier, said Victoria did not have to enter an international debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo say that women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are being denied is not right,\u8221\'20\'1D Crozier said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think Miss Patten has done the homework on this bill. She\u8217\'20\'19s just rushed it in, she\u8217\'20\'19s used some international hoo-ha in the US to bring that ideological error and whip up hysteria on an issue that just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t exist in this state. It is ridiculous and it\u8217\'20\'19s wrong.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/guardian-australia-morning-mail/2014/jun/24/-sp-guardian-australias-morning-mail-subscribe-by-email?CMP=copyembed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up to receive an email with the top stories from Guardian Australia every morning}}}\par \par \uc2 Crozier also said there was no precedent across the state where any health service had to be mandated to provide certain services.\par \par \uc2 Joining Patten in voting in support of the bill were Animal Justice MP Andy Meddick, the Victorian Greens leader, Samantha Ratnam, Sustainable Australia MP Clifford Hayes, Transport Matters MP Rod Barton, Justice Party MPs Stuart Grimley and Tania Maxwell.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/aug/16/crossbench-mp-prepares-to-wedge-victorian-government-on-abortion-access-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported on Tuesday}}}\uc2  that Patten had been unable to secure a commitment from the government that they would look at the issue before Victorians head to the polls in November, at a time it\u8217\'20\'19s understood that at least one patient a week is being turned away when seeking a termination at a denominational hospital in the state.\par \par \uc2 The majority of abortions in Victoria are performed in dedicated private clinics and private hospitals, which can present a barrier to access for some.\par \par \uc2 Under Patten\u8217\'20\'19s bill, all Victorian hospitals that receive public funding \u8211\'20\'13 including those with religious affiliations \u8211\'20\'13 would be legally required to provide advice and services for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraceptive treatment. But the bill would not change the right of individual doctors to refuse to perform abortions on religious grounds.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/abortion-in-victoria"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Under the current laws}}}\uc2 , pregnant women can access abortions up to 24 weeks in Victoria. Beyond 24 weeks, a medical practitioner can provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if another medical practitioner agrees that it is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 A doctor with a conscientious objection to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required to refer any pregnant woman inquiring about termination information or services to another doctor who does not object.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year the Victorian premier, \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2 , said abortions were a matter between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220817ei8h000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
