\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How they won: Kansas organizers unpack their big win for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2022\par 17:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strategies that worked in Kansas \u8211\'20\'13 countering misinformation, building a broad coalition \u8211\'20\'13 offer lessons for other ballot measures\par \par \uc2 In February, long before organizers in Kansas had made the hundreds of thousands of calls, knocked on the tens of thousands of doors; or did the thousands of media interviews needed to win a monumental race against an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment, they started having parties.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes they were small parties: parties where tea and cookies were handed out, and people sat in living rooms getting to know one another. Other times, they sat around a dinner table, drinking wine with strangers.\par \par \uc2 And always, there were stories.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll over the state, people talked about how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had impacted their lives or a loved one\u8217\'20\'19s. They were honest with family and friends for the first time in many cases, combatting misinformation and fundraising while they did it. And soon those conversations spread,\u8221\'20\'1D said Emily Wales, the president of Planned Parenthood Great Plains, which covers Kansas.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s just one tactic used in a campaign that delivered a stunning win for pro-choice advocates in the US, who have faced years of setbacks and shifting goalposts. Long before Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturn and the scrapping of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, courts in the US have been stacked; legal definitions of life and personhood have been altered; and civil apparatus has been created, allowing individuals to put huge bounties on the heads of those involved in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 So, despite such a long, careful campaign, it was hard to be certain of victory even as early results showed the no side winning on election night. Organizers became cautiously optimistic when record turnout numbers began filtering in, indicating participation closer to that of a presidential election in some counties. They were still cautiously optimistic when the first big, conservative counties, like Saline county and Sedgewick, swung overwhelmingly in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe running joke is that I\u8217\'20\'19m still cautiously optimistic,\u8221\'20\'1D Kansas for Constitutional Freedom\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson, Ashley All, said as she stood on the stage with her three children \u8211\'20\'13 after the vote to keep state protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Kansas constitution was called.\par \par \uc2 That reluctance to dare to believe they had done it was based not just on the losses of recent years, but also on an election cycle full of dirty tactics, where the no campaign knew the odds were stacked against them \u8211\'20\'13 and they had to fight hard to battle conspiracy theories and misinformation. Nothing was taken for granted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt the start, people didn\u8217\'20\'19t even know which way to vote to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,\u8221\'20\'1D says All, describing a vote that was confusing right down to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/aug/01/kansas-abortion-vote-value-them-both-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 name of the amendment}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/aug/02/kansas-abortion-ballot-language"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wording on the ballot.}}}\par \par \uc2 And then there was the misinformation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest challenge was the unwillingness to even acknowledge the current state of the law. We really had to spend a lot of time explaining how the amendment was actually about opening the door for politicians to pass a total ban,\u8221\'20\'1D says All.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe whole thing was pretty disingenuous,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , a state policy expert for the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe [yes campaign] were essentially trying to use false information, to make it sound like there is no regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kansas. Clearly, that\u8217\'20\'19s false,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, pointing to numerous restrictions in a state where there are just a handful of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and a whole host of laws restricting state funding from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and restricting its accessibility.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTypically, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have relied on fomenting stigma. They cast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as dangerous and in need of regulation. They paint patients essentially as victims. And they paint providers as unscrupulous. And that\u8217\'20\'19s powerful messaging,\u8221\'20\'1D added Nash.\par \par \uc2 So how did they win, and what does this mean for the battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US?\par \par \uc2 The win relied on an organized front that had started long before Roe was overturned. A coalition of pro-choice groups partnered up more than a year ago, including organizations such as Planned Parenthood, \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and the \uc2 League of Women Voters\uc2  in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 And they didn\u8217\'20\'19t stop at progressives: with Kansas leaning Republican (44%), with a lot of unaffiliated voters (30%), the coalition had to be broad.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe really understood that was essential to our survival,\u8221\'20\'1D says All, who worked with moderate Republican groups, and even former Republican legislators and their constituents, to get votes for Kansas for Constitutional Freedom (KCF), the pro-choice coalition.\par \par \uc2 Together that coalition raised millions, out-funding the Catholic church, which poured $3m into the \u8220\'20\'1Cyes\u8221\'20\'1D campaign.\par \par \uc2 They got the word out using a model that used the strategies typical of a congressional campaign \u8211\'20\'13 ads and mailers and phone banking \u8211\'20\'13 as well as those typical of a grassroots activist campaign, including volunteer work, door knocking and word of mouth, through events like the house parties mentioned above.\par \par \uc2 Emily Wales from Planned Parenthood believes the key to success was meeting voters where they were and being truthful: after all, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/02/22/poll-shows-kansas-residents-skeptical-banning-abortion-all-cases/6878658001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 60% of voters in Kansas did not support restrictions on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 about the total proportion who voted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-rights-referendum-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s amendment in the end}}}\uc2  (59%).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can overplay your hand, which is what the [yes campaign] did. They leaned into a lot of theatrics, and in some cases, outright deceiving messages, wholly intended to confuse people. And it undercut trust. All we had to do was be honest with voters,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wales.\par \par \uc2 In Kansas, the battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is far from over \u8211\'20\'13 with judicial retention elections, legislative elections and a governor\u8217\'20\'19s race all approaching. And with ballot initiatives like the one in Kansas coming across the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballot.org/ballot-measures-cheat-sheet/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 country}}}\uc2 , consensus-building will be crucial, campaigners say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to be willing to talk to voters across the political spectrum because the vast majority of Americans support access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 whether they are Republican or Libertarian or Democrat,\u8221\'20\'1D said KCF\u8217\'20\'19s All.\par \par \uc2 As ballot initiatives to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach in places like Colorado, Kentucky and Montana; and initiatives to protect them on the horizon in California, Vermont and Michigan, Corrine Rivera Fowler from the Ballot Initiative Strategy Center, which works to put progressive issues on the ballot, says outreach and education is going to be needed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[Some of these] are not \u8216\'20\'18direct democracy states\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D Fowler says. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople don\u8217\'20\'19t regularly vote on constitutional amendments, which can only be referred by their legislators. The process is unfamiliar to voters. The language may be unfamiliar to them. And so we expect misinformation to continue,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 In Montana she says misinformation has already begun: the ballot refers to \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical care of fetuses\u8221\'20\'1D. Meanwhile, in Colorado, citizens are trying to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the ballot for a fourth time, after failing repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to continue to be diligent about ensuring that folks understand that these constitutional amendments will likely create a clear no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protection,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 But the supreme court decision could also have been a shot in the arm for people, and the win in Kansas a much-needed piece of optimism for the pro-choice movement, providing a narrow opening for expanding access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, the Guttmacher\u8217\'20\'19s Nash says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLegislators were very ginned up. They just saw the Dobbs decision eviscerate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights across the country. But now they are being confronted by a wave of people saying: \u8216\'20\'18Actually, we want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be available.\u8217\'20\'19 So they are between a rock and a hard place,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Fowler thinks putting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a direct vote will have better results for the pro-choice movement than relying on legislators, representatives and judges to rule on their behalf.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have worked on many pieces of legislation \u8211\'20\'13 like paid family leave for employees, and payday lending interest rate caps \u8211\'20\'13 where the legislature actually refuses to pass laws. And finally, we had to run ballot measures, and we were able to pass them by large margins,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe thing is, is our elected officials are not often representing us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 12 August 2022 to correct that Colorado anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures were filed by citizens, not legislators.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220812ei8c000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
