\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 After the fall of Roe, Republican pursuit of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans appears to falter\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 1,801 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2022\par 16:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The stunning defeat of the Kansas referendum and internal divisions have undercut an all-out assault on reproductive rights\par \par \uc2 In the leadup to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturning Roe v Wade and thus scrapping federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protection, Republican lawmakers across the country maintained an uncompromising rallying cry against abortions, vowing to implement a sweeping wave of restrictions in their states.\par \par \uc2 However, since the highest court in the US overturned the ruling, many Republican leaders and officials have become more hesitant \u8211\'20\'13 or have even gone silent \u8211\'20\'13 over the exact type of bans they promised to enact.\par \par \uc2 As Republicans move towards an election season rife with internal disagreements within their own party and mixed public opinions on exceptions in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans such as instances of rape and incest, many rightwing lawmakers are finding it increasingly difficult to implement cohesive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 The phenomenon has been starkly illustrated by Kansas\u8217\'20\'19s referendum last week, where the usually reliably Republican state voted to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in its state constitution, providing an unexpected boost from red state America to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement.\par \par \uc2 With delays in passing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills across the US and contentious questions on how far the bans will reach, Republicans are now, as Sarah Longwell, a moderate Republican strategist, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/06/25/the-dog-that-caught-the-car-republicans-brace-for-the-impact-of-reversing-roe-00042387"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  to Politico, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe dog that caught the car\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2022/07/15/key-facts-about-the-abortion-debate-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  conducted between 27 June and 4 July by the \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  center, a majority of the American public disapproves of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe: 57% of adults disapprove of the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, including 43% who strongly disapprove, and 41% of American adults approve while 25% strongly approve of the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 The survey also found that 62% of Americans say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, and 36% of Americans say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in all or most cases. Only 38% of Republicans say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, marking a 1-point decrease from poll results obtained in 2007.\par \par \uc2 As Republican lawmakers grapple with mixed public opinions, many lawmakers have been divided over just how far they should go to ban abortions. With the recent case of the 10-year-old rape victim traveling across state lines from Ohio to Indiana to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continuing to dominate national headlines, many Republicans are realizing that the reality they are presented with differs vastly from their initial narratives surrounding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics.\par \par \uc2 What kind of exceptions should be made in cases of rape and incest? Should a woman be granted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if she is faced with a life-threatening ectopic pregnancy or an incomplete miscarriage? If an outright ban is put in place, should there be expansions of paid family leave benefits and increased funding for foster care and women\u8217\'20\'19s health?\par \par \uc2 Some states have plowed ahead. Indiana has now passed a Republican-sponsored bill that would ban nearly all abortions in the state with limited exceptions, including cases of rape and incest, and to protect the health of the mother. That made it the first state in the US to put new restrictions in place, rather than just rely on a pre-existing \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D passed before the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 But even in Indiana the move came after a series of thorny debates in the Indiana congress that reflect the growing divide Republicans are facing when it comes to fleshing out the specifics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban bills.\par \par \uc2 Before Roe v Wade was overturned, lawmakers did not spend \u8220\'20\'1Cenough time on those issues, because you knew it was an issue you didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to really get into the granular level in. But we\u8217\'20\'19re in there and we\u8217\'20\'19re recognizing that this is pretty hard work,\u8221\'20\'1D Republican Indiana state senator \uc2 Rodric Bray{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/27/us/indiana-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another Indiana Republican state senator, Kyle Walker, who voted against the ban last month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KyleCWalker/status/1549464441774809090?s=20&t=jWlj4xC29HtZt2CX0mYepw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CI believe we must strike a balance for pregnant women to make their own health decisions in the first trimester of the pregnancy and also provide protections for an unborn baby as it progresses toward viability outside the womb.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even state senator Sue Glick, the sponsor of the bill, said that she was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot exactly\u8221\'20\'1D happy with the bill.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the American Civil Liberties Union of Indiana {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/27/us/indiana-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called}}}\uc2  the bill \u8220\'20\'1Ccruel\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cdangerous\u8221\'20\'1D, while Indiana Right to Life {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://irtl.org/indiana-right-to-life-sb1-is-a-weak-and-troubling-bill-falls-woefully-short/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticized}}}\uc2  it as being \u8220\'20\'1Cweak and troubling\u8221\'20\'1D, saying that it \u8220\'20\'1Clacks any teeth to actually reduce abortions in Indiana by holding those who perform abortions or would intentionally skirt the law accountable with criminal consequences.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 South Dakota, a predominantly Republican state, is facing a similar situation.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the bombshell leak of the supreme court draft opinion on Roe, Republican governor \uc2 Kristi Noem{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/govkristinoem/status/1521303498545061889?s=20&t=EB5OSquWHecjxBaOOfMuQw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that she will \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediately call for a special session to save lives and guarantee that every unborn child has a right to life in South Dakota.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, since the supreme court overturned Roe, Noem has yet to publicly give any indication of when or if a special session will still take place. In response to the Associated Press asking if the special legislative session is still on the table, Noem\u8217\'20\'19s office said it will happen \u8220\'20\'1Clater this year\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Noem has largely kept her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-2022-midterm-elections-us-supreme-court-presidential-kristi-noem-d3b6fa5ff263e79aab1ef86bdfaf0860"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 language}}}\uc2  surrounding South Dakota\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans vague, simply reaffirming that \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is more work to do\u8221\'20\'1D and promising to \u8220\'20\'1Chelp mothers in crisis\u8221\'20\'1D. In June, Noem appeared to soften her approach on abortions by saying that doctors, not their patients, should be prosecuted for offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe women should ever be prosecuted,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe there should be any punishment for women, ever, that are in a crisis situation or have an unplanned pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-us-supreme-court-health-sd-state-wire-c381483c909b80e88fc27d0482e743ac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 . The governor also set up a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sd.gov/newsitem.aspx?id=30326#:~:text=PIERRE%2C%20S.D.%20%E2%80%93%20Today%2C%20Governor,%2C%20financial%20assistance%2C%20and%20adoption."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 website}}}\uc2  for pregnant women that aims to \u8220\'20\'1Chelp mothers and their babies before birth and after by providing resources for pregnancy, new parents, financial assistance and adoption.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the Associated Press, South Dakota Right to Life\u8217\'20\'19s executive director, Dale Bartscher, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-2022-midterm-elections-us-supreme-court-presidential-kristi-noem-d3b6fa5ff263e79aab1ef86bdfaf0860"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  that Noem\u8217\'20\'19s actions reflect a turning point in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn entirely new pro-life movement has just begun \u8211\'20\'13 we stand ready to serve women, the unborn and families,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reached out to Bartscher for additional comments.\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, the Republican governor, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , appears to have taken a softer approach on the issue after the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger ban immediately went into effect when Roe was overturned. Last month, Hutchinson did not confirm that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a topic on the agenda of this month\u8217\'20\'19s special session that is supposed to focus on tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 Referring to alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hutchinson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ualrpublicradio.org/local-regional-news/2022-07-07/governor-pay-raises-for-arkansas-teachers-not-on-call-for-special-session"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s come up in conversations \u8230\'20\'26 I\u8217\'20\'19ve mentioned that need. You know, what can we do more for maternal care? What can we do more for adoption services because of the increased number that\u8217\'20\'19s going to be demanding that? And so that is a potential issue \u8230\'20\'26 so just stay tuned.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In May, Hutchinson acknowledged that his state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger law would result in \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbreaking circumstances\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/22/arkansas-abortion-trigger-law-republican-governor-asa-hutchinson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 adding}}}\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Cwhenever you see that real-life circumstances like that, the debate is going to continue and the will of the people may or may not change\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2022/jul/05/changes-in-gops-abortion-view-seen/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 admitted}}}\uc2  that abortions performed in the exceptions of rape and incest are increasingly \u8220\'20\'1Creflecting the broad view of Americans\u8221\'20\'1D but acknowledged that the issue is \u8220\'20\'1Cstill a very divided [topic].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, whether Hutchinson will ask lawmakers to consider the exceptions during the state\u8217\'20\'19s upcoming legislative special session remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Ohio, the Republican governor, \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , has refused to comment on the state\u8217\'20\'19s recently enacted \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat bill\u8221\'20\'1D, which makes abortions illegal after six weeks into a pregnancy. As a result of the state\u8217\'20\'19s strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, a 10-year-old rape victim from the state had to travel to Indiana to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DeWine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2022/07/child-rape-case-ohio-draconian-abortion-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 condemned}}}\uc2  the case as a \u8220\'20\'1Chorrible, horrible tragedy\u8221\'20\'1D but did not signal whether he would amend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in the state. Speaking to reporters last month, DeWine refused to advocate for specific \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and said that he is \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing to let the debate play out a little bit\u8221\'20\'1D, referring to the legislative debate that is expected to happen in a few months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to hear from medical experts, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to hear from other people,\u8221\'20\'1D he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/local/columbus/2022/07/28/dewine-demurs-abortion-questions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 , adding: \u8220\'20\'1Cthen there\u8217\'20\'19ll be a time when I\u8217\'20\'19ll certainly weigh in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Roe got overturned, Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s top Republican lawmaker has been expressing similar sentiments to DeWine\u8217\'20\'19s. In June, Governor Glenn Youngkin told an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that he would \u8220\'20\'1Chappily and gleefully\u8221\'20\'1D sign any bill that would protect life, which he believes begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin has expressed support for a ban on abortions after 15 weeks with exceptions for rape, incest and risk to the mother\u8217\'20\'19s health.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin did not specify his support for any particular policies, although he acknowledged the divisive nature of the issue and called for a legislative process to hash out nuances in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban bills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a pro-life governor and I will sign a bill that comes to my desk that protects life and I look forward to that. But as of now, what we need is the process to start and to take the next four or five or six months and to work on a bill that can be supported on a bipartisan basis,\u8221\'20\'1D he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wavy.com/news/politics/virginia-politics/gov-glenn-youngkin-dodges-questions-on-abortion-ban-at-conception/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As Republicans across the country face a widening divide over the particularities of implementing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, a leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group has been urging Republicans in Congress not to leave the issue to the states. Many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists worry that extreme measures by Republican state lawmakers may cost Republican lawmakers seats nationally, especially with midterms on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, in the wake of the Kansas referendum result, many Democratic strategists now believe public opinion, even in many red states, will be on their side. The issue can be used to shore up under-threat Democrats and wielded as a weapon against Republican candidates who can be portrayed as out of step with most Americans.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s3.documentcloud.org/documents/22088417/dobbs-congressional-memo.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 memo}}}\uc2  from Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America sent out in July, Republican lawmakers were encouraged to stay away from phrases such as \u8220\'20\'1Cnationwide ban\u8221\'20\'1D and were urged not to relay the issue to state lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is vitally important that pro-life Members of Congress highlight the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism of Democrats, who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand, up until the moment of birth, paid for the taxpayer,\u8221\'20\'1D the memo said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220811ei8b0012x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
