\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  view on the Kansas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  vote: voice of America\b0\par\par\uc2 Editorial \par \uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par 17:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This week\u8217\'20\'19s vote to defend women\u8217\'20\'19s rights mirrors US opinion on the issue more generally and may shape the midterm elections\par \par \uc2 Nearly 20 years ago, the political writer Thomas Frank authored a bestseller to which he gave the title {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/What%27s_the_Matter_with_Kansas%3F_(book)#:~:text=How%20Conservatives%20Won%20the%20Heart,of%20Kansas%2C%20Frank%27s%20native%20state."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s the Matter with Kansas?}}}\uc2  It was one of the first books of the post-\uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  era to try to nail the rightwing populism redefining middle America. Mr Frank, who is himself from Kansas, chose the title deliberately. Despite an earlier history of grassroots antitrust activism, 20th- and 21st-century Kansas had dug in ever deeper against progressivism; no Democratic presidential candidate has now won there {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_presidential_elections_in_Kansas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since 1964}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who epitomises everything about which Mr Frank wrote, carried Kansas with ease in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of Mr Frank\u8217\'20\'19s argument was the view that culture war campaigns on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gay equality have been crucial in persuading economically insecure Kansas voters to move ever more solidly rightwards. Much of the book focuses on how the Democratic party itself contributed to the shift. The consequence of this process seemed to reach an even darker place in 2009 when the pro-choice doctor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/2009/jul/29/george-tiller-abortion-doctor-shot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 George Tiller}}}\uc2  was murdered in the Kansas city of Wichita.\par \par \uc2 Yet on Tuesday voters in Kansas chose to make a stand. In an unexpectedly high turnout contest, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they voted to uphold}}}\uc2  the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights by a 59% to 41% margin. They did this in the face of the widely held view that the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/26/the-guardian-view-on-overturning-roe-v-wade-anti-abortionists-reign-supreme"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  judgment has reset America\u8217\'20\'19s political landscape more conservatively. They also defied the expectation that Republicans, not Democrats, would be more energised by the campaign.\par \par \uc2 It is possible that there were special local factors at play in Kansas. The voting paper was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/aug/02/kansas-abortion-ballot-language"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 confusingly written}}}\uc2  ; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters had to vote \u8220\'20\'1CNo\u8221\'20\'1D not \u8220\'20\'1CYes\u8221\'20\'1D to keep the state\u8217\'20\'19s protections. Tuesday was also a day in which hardline conservatives, supporting and supported by Mr Trump, scored well in other states, such as Arizona. Caution is therefore in order in extrapolating too recklessly from the Kansas vote. Nevertheless, the vote was a rallying call. If 59% of the people can vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Kansas, the likelihood is that at least 59% will vote for them in many other states too, perhaps in at least 40 of the 50. It is also very much in line with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2022/07/06/majority-of-public-disapproves-of-supreme-courts-decision-to-overturn-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national polling}}}\uc2  showing 57% national disapproval after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling. President Biden was right to emphasise in his reaction to the Kansas vote that the majority of Americans support women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In Kansas at least, the justices have not, after all, had the last word. Most of all, this vote was important for the women of the state. But it has two wider implications. The first is that democracy has hit back, not just at the supreme court ruling, but also at the false idea that the court should have the final say in American politics. The second is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights may prove to be a potent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/aug/04/the-kansas-victory-shows-that-democrats-can-fight-for-abortion-rights-and-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mobilising issue}}}\uc2  in the November midterm elections more generally, which indeed they should be. It is high time that Democrats realised they do not have to campaign on economic issues alone and instead took the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the Republicans. The Kansas vote should embolden them to do so.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220805ei85002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
