\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Kansas victory shows that Democrats can fight for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights and win\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 951 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2022\par 15:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even in conservative Kansas, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are popular. The Democrats need to stop shying away from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and make it a campaign issue\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t even close; it was a blowout. With an unexpectedly large turnout and a huge margin, Kansas voters on Tuesday rejected a measure that would have removed the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from their state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. Nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/08/02/us/elections/results-kansas-abortion-amendment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 60% of voters}}}\uc2  in the deeply conservative state rejected the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure. Only about 40% supported it.\par \par \uc2 The so-called Value Them Both Act would have dramatically devalued women in Kansas. The bill was designed to amend the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution in response to a 2019 ruling by the state supreme court, which found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was protected in the state charter\u8217\'20\'19s guarantee of \u8220\'20\'1Cequal and inalienable rights\u8221\'20\'1D to all citizens. In contrast to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , the Kansas court rejected the idea that civil rights were frozen in time at the time of the document\u8217\'20\'19s ratification; instead, they extended those equal rights to women. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are now asked: is this declaration of rights more than an idealized aspiration?\u8221\'20\'1D the court wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd, if so, do the substantive rights include a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to make decisions about her body, including the decision whether to continue her pregnancy? We answer these questions, \u8216\'20\'18Yes.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Overwhelmingly, by a margin of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/08/02/us/elections/results-kansas-abortion-amendment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roughly 20 points}}}\uc2 , Kansan voters agreed with them.\par \par \uc2 It was the first electoral test of support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in June, and the results were unambiguous. Even in conservative Kansas, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are popular with most Americans. Even in conservative Kansas, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are offensive to them.\par \par \uc2 On paper, this shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been surprising. Americans have a wide range of opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but broadly, the notion that women and others should have a legal right to end their pregnancy is very popular, capturing somewhere between 60% and 70% support. Accordingly, ballot initiatives asking voters to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/irin/status/1554638177767489536?cxt=HHwWgIC8wfLLl5MrAAAA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tend to fail}}}\uc2 , according to New York Magazine correspondent Irin Carmon. A measure that would have granted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2011/11/08/142159280/mississippi-voters-reject-personhood-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 personhood rights}}}\uc2  to fetuses and embryos failed in very conservative Mississippi in 2011. A municipal ballot measure that aimed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/national/archive/2013/11/albuquerque-voters-soundly-reject-20-week-abortion-ban/355327/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban abortions after 20 weeks}}}\uc2  in the city of Albuquerque, New Mexico, failed by a large margin in an unusually high-turnout election in 2013. When South Dakota {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mprnews.org/story/2006/11/08/sdabortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban in 2006}}}\uc2 , pro-choice advocates managed to collect enough signatures to put the measure to a popular vote. The electorate threw that out, too.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ruling overturning Roe v Wade is particularly unpopular with the public, and has incited renewed passion for the pro-choice cause. According to polling from CNN, 63% of Americans \u8211\'20\'13 almost two-thirds \u8211\'20\'13 say they disapprove of the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling. A little more than half of them, 51%, say they disapprove \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly\u8221\'20\'1D. This public outrage is clearly translating into votes. After the court reversed Roe on 24 June, many states saw a surge in new voter registrations. In Kansas, 70% of those new voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2022/08/03/kansas-abortion-vote-democrats-opportunity/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were women}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There were some indications that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side was nervous, even before their resounding defeat on election day. They played dirty. The vote was scheduled for a blindingly hot primary day in August, when turnouts are usually low and Republicans fare better. In the days before the election, a Republican-aligned firm in Nevada {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/08/02/kansas-abortion-texts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sent out texts to Kansas voters}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWomen in Kansas are losing their choice on reproductive rights,\u8221\'20\'1D the texts read. \u8220\'20\'1CVoting YES on the Amendment will give women a choice. Vote YES to protect women\u8217\'20\'19s health.\u8221\'20\'1D A yes vote on the constitutional amendment would have been a vote against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Clearly, the anti-choice Republicans didn\u8217\'20\'19t think they could win on the merits. Turns out, they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 But you would never know how resoundingly popular \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are from the behavior of the Democrats, who for the past three decades, and since the election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  specifically, have been allergic to full-throated defenses of reproductive rights and other so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cculture war\u8221\'20\'1D issues.\par \par \uc2 The party\u8217\'20\'19s centrist leadership has made the calculation that only economic issues \u8211\'20\'13 defined, in practice, as issues that affect white men \u8211\'20\'13 can garner voters\u8217\'20\'19 enthusiasm. The Biden administration was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jul/10/biden-executive-order-abortion-white-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flat-footed and inept}}}\uc2  in its response to Dobbs, agreeing to take only the flimsiest and most risk-averse steps to restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and alienating huge swaths of its base as it attempted to keep the focus on its efforts to curb inflation. Biden hardly ever even says \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. You get the sense he would much rather not be talking about it at all.\par \par \uc2 But the results in Kansas suggest that he should be. The ballot initiative on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  produced a massive turnout. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights got considerably more electoral support than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did in most Kansas counties. It\u8217\'20\'19s a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cculture war\u8221\'20\'1D issue that brought voters out in droves to vote for a Democratic agenda item. The Kansas vote shows that the overturning of Roe has created a moral emergency that voters will respond to. Ignoring these \u8220\'20\'1Cculture war\u8221\'20\'1D issues does not make the Democrats look sensible and moderate. It makes them look like cowards, running away from a fight.\par \par \uc2 What Republicans want to do to America, especially with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is unpopular. More importantly, it is anti-democratic and immoral. This is a fight that the voters want to take on. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for the Democratic party to join them.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220804ei84001h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
