\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Kansas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  referendum drives record number of voters to polls\b0\par\par\uc2 Alvin Chang \par \uc2 433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2022\par 14:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly half of state\u8217\'20\'19s registered voters turned out in first electoral test of US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 Most primary elections turn out only a small portion of the electorate \u8211\'20\'13 but this year\u8217\'20\'19s Kansas primary was unlike any other in the state.\par \par \uc2 This year\u8217\'20\'19s primary ballot included a question to decide whether to stop the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution from protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, which could have paved the way for the state legislature to completely ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Kansans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-rights-referendum-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resoundingly voted against}}}\uc2  the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure \u8211\'20\'13 and turned out in record numbers to do so.\par \par \uc2 With 95% of precincts reporting, more than 900,000 voters turned out for the primary election, which accounts for nearly half of registered voters. It\u8217\'20\'19s the kind of turnout the state usually sees in general elections.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, the Kansas primary was the first electoral test of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the country.\par \par \uc2 Not only was turnout high, but before the election Kansans were also registering to vote in record numbers. As of July 2022, weeks after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade, about 1.9 million people registered to vote \u8211\'20\'13 comparable only to the 2020 general election, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was vying for a second term.\par \par \uc2 A typical primary election draws only the most engaged citizens because they\u8217\'20\'19re only choosing which candidate will represent each political party in the general election. Turnout in primaries is usually between 20% and 30% of registered voters.\par \par \uc2 More than 47% of registered voters participated on Tuesday, with tens of thousands of votes still to be counted.\par \par \uc2 This comes after a concerted Republican effort to sway the vote and clear the path for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Republican state legislature put this measure on the primary ballot, rather than the November general election ballot, probably because they knew primary voters tend to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/02/22/poll-shows-kansas-residents-skeptical-banning-abortion-all-cases/6878658001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more conservative}}}\uc2  than the overall electorate. In addition, the language Republicans put on the ballot was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/aug/02/kansas-abortion-ballot-language"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 confusing and misleading}}}\uc2 . For example, the ballot language says the amendment would stop state funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though the state already bans public funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fhsu.edu/docking/Kansas-Speaks/2021-kansas-speaks-report-final_1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll}}}\uc2  in late 2021 found that more than 60% of Kansans oppose making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely illegal \u8211\'20\'13 44% of the state\u8217\'20\'19s voters are registered Republicans and 26% are registered Democrats. With nearly all the votes counted on Wednesday, about 59% of voters said they did not want to remove the state constitution\u8217\'20\'19s protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220803ei83003h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
