\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We could feel it\u8217\'20\'19: Kansans celebrate upset  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights victory\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor in Kansas City \par \uc2 1,137 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par 15:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Organizers said treating reproductive rights as a non-partisan issue was key to success in a Republican-leaning state\par \par \uc2 In a conference room at the Sheraton in the Kansas City suburb of Overland Park, people screamed, whooped, cheered and cried as a vote to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Kansas\u8217\'20\'19s state constitution came down late on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 And it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just Democrats.\par \par \uc2 James Quigley, 72, a retired doctor and a Republican from Johnson county, sat on his own drinking a glass of white wine after hearing the news. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a much more nuanced issue than anti-choice individuals would have you think,\u8221\'20\'1D he told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is deeply personal, sometimes tragic, but also sometimes a liberating decision \u8211\'20\'13 and we should trust women, their physicians, and their God on that,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe could feel it \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19ve been feeling it for weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marcia Corbett, 71, a swing voter and local business owner, before the vote came in.\par \par \uc2 The result had been eagerly awaited, as Kansas was the first state in the country to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on the ballot since Roe v Wade, which federally guaranteed them, was overturned by the supreme court. It came after weeks of uncertainty, in a race in which misinformation abounded and tactics got ugly.\par \par \uc2 The victory \u8211\'20\'13 and its sheer scale in a usually reliably Republican and socially conservative state like Kansas \u8211\'20\'13 has sent shockwaves through the United States and provided a shot in the arm for efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights under siege across America.\par \par \uc2 In Kansas, that fight had gotten dirty. On Tuesday, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/08/02/kansas-abortion-texts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 former Republican congressman}}}\uc2  was linked to messages targeting voters with an anonymous, misleading text encouraging people to vote yes to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 when in fact a yes vote would have overturned a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Vandals also spray-painted the walls of a Catholic church weeks earlier, with the phrase, \u8220\'20\'1CMy body, my choice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nor had victory seemed certain on the day of the vote.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, as voting began, the mood seemed amicable in Douglas county on a hot, sticky day, where temperatures consistently threatened to hit the hundreds. Polling booths in Lawrence and Eudora saw a steady drip of voters, even in the middle of the day, with dozens of voters lining up to vote at any given time. Many were unaffiliated, but turned up just to vote in the referendum.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re a Republican state. And we don\u8217\'20\'19t generally vote that way\par \par \uc2 Patrick Perry\par \par \uc2 At the Eudora community center in Douglas county, Patrick Perry, 43, a mechanic and registered Republican, said he was voting no. A veteran who had fought in Iraq, he said he was voting due to his own \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal circumstances\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 his wife needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a medical emergency during their marriage, in a pregnancy that would have otherwise taken her life. But he didn\u8217\'20\'19t expect Kansas to side with him. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re a Republican state,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we don\u8217\'20\'19t generally vote that way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But on a night of huge turnout, Kansas voted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution, with the no vote securing a whopping 59% to 41% of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 At the beginning of the night, the mood had been cautiously hopeful at the Kansas for Constitutional Freedom event in Overland Park, with the no vote ahead from the start. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in the lead, and no is better than yes!\u8221\'20\'1D a young girl said to her mother, from next-door Missouri. The two had been canvassing together for weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2  stood up to speak early in the night, telling the audience of about 100 people: \u8220\'20\'1CThe [supreme court] decision definitely felt like a gut punch to a lot of people in our community \u8230\'20\'26 But we stood up and got to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Following speeches, all eyes in the room were on a television projection blaring MSNBC\u8217\'20\'19s elections statistics guru Steve Kornacki, whose voice was barely audible over the sounds of people chattering, drinking and bursting into cheers whenever a county\u8217\'20\'19s no vote was called.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CImagine how good we are going to feel when we beat the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement and the Republicans, who lied at every turn,\u8221\'20\'1D state congresswoman Stephanie Clayton said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel really good right now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Leslie Butsch, who had tears in her eyes by 8.30pm. She was watching as the vote in Johnson county first showed signs of leaning heavily towards no, after weeks of spending her evenings knocking on doors there. An hour later, when the result came through, she was one of the few people without a celebratory drink in her hand \u8211\'20\'13 she\u8217\'20\'19d just spent all her cash tipping the bar staff in a flurry of happiness.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel overwhelmed with gratitude. Today we learned that organizers are more powerful than ever. We did the impossible,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 State senator Dinah Sykes burst into tears when the vote was called, covering her mouth and showing friends goosebumps on her arms. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just amazing. It\u8217\'20\'19s breathtaking that women\u8217\'20\'19s voices were heard and [that] we care about women\u8217\'20\'19s health,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She knew that the vote would be close in a state that gave Trump a 15 percentage point lead over Biden in the 2020 election. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we were close in a lot of rural areas and that really made the difference \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19m just so grateful,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Ashley All, the spokesperson for KCF, said the success of their campaign was testament to non-partisanship \u8211\'20\'13 and other states should take heed. \u8220\'20\'1CIt will be interesting for other states to watch this, and see this is not a partisan issue,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made a statement on the result late on Tuesday. \u8220\'20\'1CVoters in Kansas turned out in record numbers to reject extreme efforts to amend the state constitution to take away a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose and open the door for a statewide ban,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis vote makes clear what we know: the majority of Americans agree that women should have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and should have the right to make their own healthcare decisions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the defeated anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Kansans for Life sent out an email to supporters following the vote, sharing their dismay. For a movement that has been on the rise in America \u8211\'20\'13 since before Roe was overturned, and after \u8211\'20\'13 it was clear they had suffered a powerful blow.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe mainstream media propelled the left\u8217\'20\'19s false narrative, contributing to the confusion that misled Kansans about the amendment,\u8221\'20\'1D it said, and vowed to fight on. \u8220\'20\'1COur movement and campaign have proven our resolve and commitment. We will not abandon women and babies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220803ei83002e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
