\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Victorian public hospitals could not prevent doctors from providing abortions under new bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Tamsin Rose \par \uc2 597 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 July 2022\par 21:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Reason party leader \uc2 Fiona Patten\uc2 , who is introducing the bill, says \u8216\'20\'18imposed religious faith has no place in the public health system\u8217\'20\'19Follow our Australia news live blog for the latest updatesGet our free news app, morning email briefing or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 Public hospitals in Victoria would not be allowed to stop doctors from providing abortions under a bill being introduced to state parliament this week by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/apr/29/i-am-taking-this-personally-victorian-crossbencher-fiona-patten-bemused-by-federal-labor-preference-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crossbench MP Fiona Patten.}}}\par \par \uc2 The legislation follows the overturning of the landmark {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Roe v Wade decision}}}\uc2  in the US in June, and comes with just four more sitting weeks before Victorians head to the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 The bill will seek to strengthen the rights of Victorians to access abortions and contraceptive services by making sure they were available at every hospital that accepts government money, including denominational hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Patten said the public health system was \u8220\'20\'1Cmistreating those who fund it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPublicly funded hospitals and other health institutions have no right to refuse these legally enshrined rights that a woman has control over her body and reproductive health,\u8221\'20\'1D Patten said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CReligion is a blessing to many amid the mysteries and vagaries of existence, but imposed religious faith has no place in the public health system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The bill would not change the right of individual medical professionals to refuse to perform abortions on religious grounds.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/abortion-in-victoria"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Under the current laws}}}\uc2 , pregnant people can access abortions up to 24 weeks in Victoria. Beyond 24 weeks, a medical practitioner can provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if another medical practitioner agrees that it is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 A doctor with a conscientious objection to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required to refer any pregnant person inquiring about termination information or services to another doctor who does not object.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals that receive public money, including those with religious affiliations, are not required to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The majority of abortions in Victoria are performed in dedicated private clinics and private hospitals, which can present a barrier to access for some.\par \par \uc2 Patten said institutions should not be able to claim \u8220\'20\'1Cconscientious objection\u8221\'20\'1D and that the bill would ensure public hospitals were not able to prevent a doctor from performing legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, last month refused to discuss reforms to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was available in public hospitals across the country in the wake of the US decision, instead insisting it was a matter for states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t control the health system. The states control the health system,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re fortunate that in Australia we don\u8217\'20\'19t have the sort of divisive debate that has occurred in the US that we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen playing out with a Supreme Court decision on Roe v Wade.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Patten, the leader of the Reason party, said the response from Albanese was \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a man who only recently decried the appalling decision of the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 ... to return \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to state governments, many of which immediately stamped on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Patten said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, my legislation is a state response. It\u8217\'20\'19s based on first principles and can and should be supported across the political spectrum.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year the Victorian premier, \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2 , said abortions were \u8220\'20\'1Cmatters \u8230\'20\'26 between a woman and her doctor\u8221\'20\'1D after MP Bernie Finn posted on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  about \u8220\'20\'1Cpraying\u8221\'20\'1D for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be banned in Australia.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian parliament returns on Tuesday after a six-week winter break.\par \par \uc2 Patten will do her second reading of the bill on Wednesday before it is debated in a fortnight.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220731ei7v00239\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
