\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democratic cities in Texas push to blunt impact of state\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam in San Antonio \par \uc2 656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2022\par 07:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Blue cities aim to pass bills to protect those who receive and provide abortions: \u8216\'20\'18The fight starts locally\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Across Texas, Democratic-held cities are galvanizing to mitigate the effects of the Republican-run state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  voted in June to overturn Roe v Wade, the landmark case that gave Americans a constitutional right to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s capital, Austin, voted last week to \u8220\'20\'1Cdecriminalize\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the city by passing the Guarding the Right to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care for Everyone (Grace) Act. Although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still illegal in the state, the passing of the Grace Act will redirect the city\u8217\'20\'19s budget to focus on going after more important crimes such as sexual assault, theft and burglary.\par \par \uc2 Local politicians in Waco, a city halfway between Austin and Dallas, followed suit and put forward their own version of the Grace Act for consideration.\par \par \uc2 Other cities such as San Antonio are also gearing up to protect those who receive and provide abortions. On Wednesday, the mayor, \uc2 Ron Nirenberg\uc2 , and the city council gathered on the steps of city hall to announce the consideration of a similar resolution in support of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 City council member Teri Castillo, who drafted a resolution, told the Guardian: \u8220\'20\'1CThe community and the constituency of San Antonio aren\u8217\'20\'19t ready to take us back in history, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why I joined those residents who took it to the streets to protest the overturning of Roe v Wade.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re seeking to do is to let folks know that the fight starts locally, and that we do have an authority as a city council to ask and give the policy recommendation that city funds will not be used to store or catalog any report of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , miscarriage or other reproductive healthcare acts strictly for the purpose of pursuing a criminal investigation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Castillo said she anticipates the Republican party will identify ways to close potential loopholes or protections for those who wish or need to seek access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to Cynthia Alkon, a Texas A&M law professor, Castillo is right to worry. Alkon underscored that de-prioritizing the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the same thing as decriminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the attorney general in Texas is really hellbent on prosecuting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related cases as crimes in counties where the local district attorney is rightfully exercising their discretionary authority and deciding not to, I think they\u8217\'20\'19re kind of left with two choices: they either try and get appointed as attorney pro tem [for the time being] or, they change the laws of what the attorney general can do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Liberal cities face an uphill battle, with several obstacles along the way, including Texas\u8217\'20\'19s hardline attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who has indicated he will pursue prosecution.\par \par \uc2 In Fort Worth\u8217\'20\'19s Tarrant county, district attorney Sharen Wilson said she will prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases, but in nearby Dallas county, her counterpart, John Creuzot, has said he will not stand in the way of anyone seeking healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Kim Ogg, district attorney of Harris county, the largest county in Houston, said she will consider prosecution in the event of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a case-by-case basis, but said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe criminalization of reproductive health will cause great harm to women in America; prosecutors and police have no role in matters between doctors and patients.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Additionally, the commissioner\u8217\'20\'19s court of Harris county, voted 3-2 in favor of Texans\u8217\'20\'19 access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception.\par \par \uc2 The so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat law\u8221\'20\'1D, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  around six weeks \u8211\'20\'13 when a cardiac activity is detected \u8211\'20\'13 is already in effect in the state. Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D, which will classify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a felony, even in the case of rape or incest, goes into effect on 25 August.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220729ei7t00109\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
