\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Will  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans deny critical training to the next generation of ob-gyns?\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,286 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2022\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, ob-gyn residency programs may need to offer out-of-state training options in order to maintain their accredited status\par \par \uc2 It was the moment Dani Mathisen had eagerly awaited for weeks. She and her husband watched the ultrasound screen as the wand slicked through the jelly on her stomach. As a doctor, Dani could interpret the images on the 20-week week anatomy scan herself \u8211\'20\'13 and immediately, she started seeing the problems in her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Her daughter\u8217\'20\'19s feet weren\u8217\'20\'19t forming quite right. A bone surgery can fix that, she thought.\par \par \uc2 There was only one kidney. But you only need one kidney.\par \par \uc2 A little bubble at the base of the spine indicated neural tube defects. A neurosurgeon could fix that. Scoliosis curved the spine itself. An orthopedic surgeon could fix that.\par \par \uc2 And then they got to the brain. It had never developed; there were no identifiable structures.\par \par \uc2 A surgeon can\u8217\'20\'19t fix that, she thought, soaked in sorrow. No one can fix that.\par \par \uc2 Dani had always wanted to be two things in life: a good doctor and a good mother. Both goals seemed to be on track. She was finishing up med school, and she and her husband bought a house with an extra bedroom that would be perfect for the nursery. They called their growing fetus Mini.\par \par \uc2 But looking at the ultrasound images, she knew that being a good doctor and a good mom meant terminating the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Dani lived in Texas, though, where abortions were banned after six weeks starting in September \u8211\'20\'13 the same month she had her ultrasound. She was finally able to book an appointment in another state, with a doctor she\u8217\'20\'19d never met before. Her husband wasn\u8217\'20\'19t allowed in the clinic with her because of Covid restrictions, and she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t keep her phone with her. The procedure took 14 hours.\par \par \uc2 After her experience, Dani, a lifelong Texan, moved far from home to do her medical residency in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are protected \u8211\'20\'13 partly in case she ever needs care like this again, but more importantly so that she can become a \u8220\'20\'1Cfull, rounded ob-gyn that has every single tool in her arsenal\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. The reversal of reproductive rights by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  in June only reinforced the decision.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans first and foremost affect pregnant people who need care. But bans proliferating through US states will also reverberate through an entire generation of healthcare professionals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to change the way that medicine is practiced,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kavita Vinekar, assistant clinical professor at the David Geffen School of Medicine at UCLA and coauthor of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.lww.com/greenjournal/Fulltext/9900/Projected_Implications_of_Overturning_Roe_v_Wade.449.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  on how restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  affects medical training. Instead of offering care based on \u8220\'20\'1Cclinical decision-making, sound evidence, and the body of knowledge that we have in medicine, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to be forced to function in an environment that\u8217\'20\'19s really wrought with confusion and fear\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Learning about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is not required in US med schools, but some programs offer lectures or opportunities to shadow clinicians. Ob-gyn residency programs are the only ones {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/pfassets/programresources/220_obgyn_abortion_training_clarification.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 required}}}\uc2 , by a national accreditation council, to train doctors on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 including procedures, counseling, and the management of complications \u8211\'20\'13 though residents may opt out of performing the procedures if they choose. Ob-gyn residency programs in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans may need to offer out-of-state training options or alternative training in order to maintain their accredited status, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/pfassets/reviewandcomment/220_obstetricsandgynecology_2022-06-24_rc.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guidelines}}}\uc2  that are being updated after the supreme court decision.\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19re going to raise a generation of ob-gyns who have had no exposure to these kinds of procedures at all\par \par \uc2 Danielle Jones\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re going to raise a generation of ob-gyns who have had no exposure to these kinds of procedures at all,\u8221\'20\'1D said Danielle Jones, an ob-gyn and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mamadoctorjones.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 science communicator}}}\uc2  who trained and lived most of her life in Texas before leaving the state about six months ago.\par \par \uc2 Physicians who don\u8217\'20\'19t feel capable or confident in their ability to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care may see their skills limited in other areas of care as well. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of what we learn in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care extends far beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,\u8221\'20\'1D Vinekar said. Many of the procedures are the same for other reproductive health complications, such as miscarriage management or uterine evacuations. \u8220\'20\'1CThose are all situations that come up whether or not you have legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in your state, and physicians need to be well-trained and able to address these sometimes emergent scenarios.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG) \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cwill be working with those states specifically to address and ensure adequate training\u8221\'20\'1D, said co-chief executive officer, Dr \uc2 Maureen Phipps\uc2 , in a press briefing about the Dobbs decision. But, she cautioned, \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s not straightforward. We are going to have to do a lot of work to ensure that training.\u8221\'20\'1D The organization will also offer resources and answer individual questions from members about the patchwork of state laws, she said.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SecBecerra/status/1546587520443482113"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged}}}\uc2  to protect the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk. But pregnancy, especially in the US, is already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/27/roe-v-wade-overturned-maternal-mortality-rate-will-rise"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extremely dangerous}}}\uc2 . Doctors fear an environment where they need to wait for patients to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jul/13/texas-abortion-ban-maternal-health-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at death\u8217\'20\'19s door}}}\uc2  before providing them the treatment they need \u8211\'20\'13 especially if they need to seek authorization in situations where a few minutes can mean the difference between life and death.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In Texas, Dani saw how close pregnant patients needed to be to death before a doctor could intervene. On her med school rotations, she would see patients with dangerously high blood pressure \u8211\'20\'13 putting them at risk for strokes and neurological damage \u8211\'20\'13 or who were clearly developing sepsis, a life-threatening infection, but whose doctors were required to wait until their conditions worsened before terminating the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou know the bad things that can happen, and you just have to sit and wait until it\u8217\'20\'19s bad enough? That\u8217\'20\'19s horrible,\u8221\'20\'1D Dani said. \u8220\'20\'1CTo me, that goes against \u8216\'20\'18do no harm\u8217\'20\'19 because it\u8217\'20\'19s happening \u8211\'20\'13 the harm is happening. It\u8217\'20\'19s just not harmful enough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Staying pregnant herself would have opened up Dani to potential harm, while the outcome for Mini would have been the same no matter what happened. All Dani could do was ease the suffering.\par \par \uc2 An undeveloped brain meant the baby would die before delivery or soon after birth, because there would be no respiratory drive prompting her to breathe. If Mini survived childbirth, she would slowly suffocate to death \u8211\'20\'13 and there was no way to save her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt wasn\u8217\'20\'19t really a decision because no one would choose to have their child live and die like that. It would have been a very painful death where she eventually just runs out of oxygen,\u8221\'20\'1D Dani said.\par \par \uc2 And watching Mini die would have made it difficult or impossible for Dani to work as a doctor. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think I would have been able to go back into medicine after watching my child die,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would not be able to relive that every time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Doctors forced to watch patients enduring such danger and trauma are going to struggle with plummeting morale and burnout, Vinekar said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can think of nothing more likely to drive people out of healthcare because of that powerlessness,\u8221\'20\'1D Vinekar said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason we\u8217\'20\'19re in this field is to help people.\u8221\'20\'1D But \u8220\'20\'1Cforcing people to incur the risk of pregnancy against their will is perhaps one of the most inhumane things you can do\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220718ei7i0012x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
