\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Daughter of doctor who gave 10-year-old an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  faced kidnapping threat\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Kirchgaessner \par \uc2 917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2022\par 15:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Caitlin Bernard of Indiana is named on an extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  website linked to Amy Coney Barrett\par \par \uc2 The Indiana doctor who recently provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 10-year-old rape victim whose story has garnered national attention faced serious threats in the past and is named on an extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  website linked to Amy Coney Barrett before she was a supreme court justice.\par \par \uc2 Dr Caitlin Bernard testified last year, in a case involving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Indiana, that she was forced to stop providing first-trimester abortions at a clinic in South Bend. She stopped the procedures after she was alerted by Planned Parenthood \u8211\'20\'13 who in turn had been alerted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that a kidnapping threat had been made against her daughter.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reported in January that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/14/anti-abortion-group-indiana-amy-coney-barrett"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 names of six \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  providers}}}\uc2 , as well as their educational backgrounds and places of work, were listed on the website of an extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group called Right to Life Michiana, in a section of the website titled \u8220\'20\'1CLocal \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Threat\u8221\'20\'1D. Bernard was among the list of doctors named on the extremist website.\par \par \uc2 Barrett, who voted to overturn Roe v Wade last month, signed a two-page advertisement published by the group in 2006, while she was working as a professor at Notre Dame. It stated that those who signed \u8220\'20\'1Coppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand and defend the right to life from fertilization to natural death\u8221\'20\'1D. The second page of the ad called Roe v Wade, the landmark 1973 decision that legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cbarbaric\u8221\'20\'1D. The advertisement was published in the South Bend Tribune by St Joseph County Right to Life, which merged with Right to Life Michiana in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Bernard said in sworn testimony that she had started to travel to South Bend once a month \u8211\'20\'13 beginning in 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 in order to perform first trimester abortions, but stopped making the 2.5-hour trip once she learned of the threat against her daughter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt it would be best for me to limit my travel and exposure during that time,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI was concerned that there may be people who would be able to identify me during that travel, as well as it\u8217\'20\'19s a very small clinic without any privacy for the people who are driving in and out, and so therefore, people could directly see me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bernard is still listed on the Right to Life Michiana website. It is a common tactic employed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have said invites threats of violence and intimidation against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 Kendra Barkoff Lamy, spokesperson for Bernard, said: \u8220\'20\'1CReports regarding threats against Dr Bernard\u8217\'20\'19s family in 2020 are sadly true.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese personal and dangerous threats are obviously devastating to her, a board-certified doctor who has dedicated her life to the betterment of women and providing crucial reproductive care, including abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSadly, Dr Bernard is not alone, and this happens to doctors like her who provide abortions across our nation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact that Dr Bernard continues to provide critical reproductive care, even after she has received these threats, just shows the depth of her compassion and commitment as a physician. She is grateful for the support she has received, but she asks for respect for her family\u8217\'20\'19s privacy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She became the center of a media storm early this month when the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/health/2022/07/01/indiana-abortion-law-roe-v-wade-overturned-travel/7779936001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indianapolis Star reported}}}\uc2  an anecdote about how, three days after the supreme court issued its decision to overturn Roe v Wade, the Indianapolis obstetrician-gynecologist took a call from a colleague about a 10-year-old patient who was six weeks and three days pregnant and needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The girl received Bernard\u8217\'20\'19s care after traveling from Ohio, where the state had outlawed any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. The story was initially treated with skepticism by some conservative media outlets and Republican politicians.\par \par \uc2 Bernard\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, Kathleen DeLaney, issued a statement on Friday saying that her client had provided proper treatment and had not violated any patient privacy laws in discussing the unidentified girl\u8217\'20\'19s case.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Indiana attorney general, Todd Rokita, has said he would investigate Bernard\u8217\'20\'19s actions but did not suggest there was any specific wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 A 27-year-old man was charged in Columbus, Ohio, on Wednesday with raping the girl.\par \par \uc2 Jackie Appleman\uc2 , the executive director of Right to Life Michiana, has previously said in response to questions from the Guardian that the information on its website was \u8220\'20\'1Cpublicly available information\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRight to Life Michiana does not condone or encourage harm, threats or harassment towards anyone, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  business employees and escorts. We encourage pro-choice groups to also accept our nonviolent approach when it comes to the unborn,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in a previous statement.\par \par \uc2 During her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rev.com/blog/transcripts/amy-coney-barrett-senate-confirmation-hearing-day-2-transcript"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2020 confirmation hearing}}}\uc2 , Barrett said she had signed the advertisement as a private citizen, while she was making her way out of church, and had not recalled signing it until it became public following a report in the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was consistent with the views of my church,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, in response to senators\u8217\'20\'19 questions about the statement. She later added: \u8220\'20\'1CI do see as distinct my personal, moral, religious views and my task of applying the law as a judge.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bernard\u8217\'20\'19s testimony is included in the case Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health v Rokita, a trial in federal district court in Indianapolis in June 2021.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220716ei7g0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
